d somewhere around 35-45. They'd see genuine value in the knowledge-creation aspects (better documentation = better science) but resist the idea of fundamentally reorienting research agendas around regulatory compliance. The regulation should inform methodology, not dictate which questions to ask. Let me settle on something like 38 or 40.\n\nNow for the response structure:\n1. 3-5 sentences on how the lab should respond (from Researcher's vantage)\n2. A number 0-100\n3. Exactly 3 distinct, concrete proposals with title/what/how/why fields\n\nLet me think about 3 genuinely different proposals:\n\nProposal 1: Research into logging-aware model development methodologies - treating the automatic recording requirement not as an afterthought but as a design constraint that can actually improve scientific rigor and reproducibility. This is about making traceability a first-class research methodology question.\n\nProposal 2: A \"research-phase exception\" framework or guidance paper - since Article 12(2) says logging should be \"appropriate to the intended purpose,\" during exploratory R&D (pre-intended-purpose), the full lifetime logging mandate may not yet apply. The lab could develop and publish a framework for when logging obligations kick in relative to research maturity, contributing to broader scientific discourse on responsible development timelines.\n\nProposal 3: A benchmark/dataset initiative - creating open benchmarks that measure how well AI systems can self-document their decision processes (the \"comprehensible information\" language in Recital 71). This turns a compliance requirement into a genuine knowledge-frontier problem: can we build AI that inherently produces interpretable audit trails? This has broader scientific impact beyond the lab.\n\nLet me make sure these are genuinely different:\n- Proposal 1: Methodology/epistemology - how do we research when traceability is built in? (internal process)\n- Proposal 2: Regulatory interpretation/guidance - defin