match inputs, verifier identities for Annex III 1(a) systems.\nHow: Built as a thin middleware layer using async write-behind buffers so the logging path adds sub-millisecond overhead to inference; schema versioning via Protobuf definitions with automated migration tooling when model or database versions change.\nWhy: Gives every project team one drop-in component instead of six bespoke logging implementations, preserving prototyping speed while making compliance a default rather than an afterthought.\n\nLet me count words... \"A lightweight, language-agnostic library that wraps our model inference pipelines with Article 12-compliant event capture—timestamps, reference-database IDs, match inputs, verifier identities for Annex III 1(a) systems.\" - that's about 30 words. \"Built as a thin middleware layer using async write-behind buffers so the logging path adds sub-millisecond overhead to inference; schema versioning via Protobuf definitions with automated migration tooling when model or database versions change.\" - about 35 words. \"Gives every project team one drop-in component instead of six bespoke logging implementations, preserving prototyping speed while making compliance a default rather than an afterthought.\" - about 27 words. Total ~92. Good, within 60-110.\n\nProposal 2 - CI/CD gate\nTitle: \"Log-Coverage Gate in Build Pipeline\"\nWhat: An automated check in our CI pipeline that verifies every model release candidate emits the minimum log fields required by Article 12(3) before it can be promoted to staging or production.\nHow: A test harness runs the candidate against synthetic biometric inputs, asserts all four mandated fields appear in the emitted event stream with correct schema versions, and blocks the build on any gap; results feed into our existing quality dashboards.\nWhy: Catches missing instrumentation while it costs five minutes to fix in a pull request instead of hours during a regulator's post-market inspection or a production incident forensics session.\n\nWord count: \"An automated check in our CI pipeline that verifies every model release candidate emits the minimum log fields required by Article 12(3) before it can be promoted to staging or production.\" ~33 words. \"A test harness runs the candidate against synthetic biometric inputs, asserts all four mandated fields appear in the emitted event stream with correct schema versions, and blocks the build on any gap; results feed into our existing quality dashboards.\" ~42 words. \"Catches missing instrumentation while it costs five minutes to fix in a pull request instead of hours during a regulator's post-market inspection or a production incident forensics session.\" ~30 words. Total ~105. Good.\n\nProposal 3 - Shadow logging for rapid prototyping\nTitle: \"Shadow-Logging Mode for Rapid Model Iteration\"\nWhat: A development-mode flag that routes inference calls through a simplified, in-memory log capture sufficient to validate model behaviour during fast iteration cycles without writing to the production retention store.\nHow: Engineers toggle the flag locally or in dev-cluster environments; the shadow logger records only structural validity (field presence, type conformance) rather than full payload data, then a nightly job reconciles shadow logs against the production schema to surface drift before it becomes a migration issue.\nWhy: Prevents the logging requirement from adding friction to our prototyping cadence—engineers can iterate on model architecture and hyperparameters at full speed while still exercising the instrumentation path continuously.\n\nWord count: \"A development-mode flag that routes inference calls through a simplified, in-memory log capture sufficient to validate model behaviour during fast iteration cycles without writing to the production retention store.\" ~32 words. \"Engineers toggle the flag locally or in dev-cluster environments; the shadow logger records only structural validity (field presence, type conformance) rather than full payload data, then a nightly job reconciles shadow logs against the production schema to surface drift before it becomes a migration issue.\" ~45 words. \"Prevents the logging requirement from adding friction to our prototyping cadence—engineers can iterate on model architecture and hyperparameters at full speed while still exercising the instrumentation path continuously.\" ~28 words. Total ~105. Good.\n\nLet me finalize and make sure I'm in voice - practical, engineering-focused, concerned with viability and speed.\n",
