d to massive fines (up to 3% of global turnover or €15M). Ensuring that engineers don't \"forget\" logging until the end of the project is a strong risk mitigation strategy.\n    *   *Weaknesses:* The shadow logger doesn't actually record the *events* required by Article 12 (it only records metadata about the logs). But since it's for \"development-mode,\" this is acceptable as long as the final product has full logging.\n\n    *   The proposal isn't trying to bypass the law in production; it's creating a development tool to ensure that when the system *is* in production, the logging works perfectly.\n    *   It solves the \"friction\" problem that often leads engineers to disable or ignore compliance requirements during R&D, which usually results in a panicked, buggy implementation right before launch.\n    *   By validating schema conformance and field presence early, it ensures the *technical capability* (required by Art 12(1)) is built-in from the start.\n\n    *   Rationale: This proposal mitigates the risk of deployment failures by integrating logging instrumentation into the dev cycle without hindering R&D speed, ensuring that production systems are compliant upon launch.\n    *   Decision: Approve.\n    *   Promise: High (e.g., 85-90). It's a sound engineering practice to ensure compliance goals are met.",
