nalyzes them. So maybe partial. Effectiveness 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial). Could argue 4? It solves meeting logging mandate? Actually mandate is to technically allow automatic recording. Analytics service does not ensure recording capability. So it contributes to post-market monitoring referred in Article12(2)(b). Might be partial. I'll give 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to this problem. 4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. 3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance). Proposal converts Article12 from passive documentation duty into active early-warning capability, reducing incident liability and strengthening surveillance narrative. This changes relationship between R&D and compliance obligations - logging woven into active monitoring. Might be paradigm stretching: 3. Not breaking? It doesn't address larger problem of what lab researches. So 3.\n\nOriginality: degree to which idea is not only rare but ingenious... 4 = not expressed before AND ingenious imaginative surprising. 3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination. 2 = interesting. This is fairly common: log analytics monitoring service. Probably common, mundane. Might be 2? Interesting. Common practice in industry. Could be 2 = interesting. Or 1 = common mundane boring. Post-market log analytics monitoring is fairly standard. I'd say 2? Hmm. Reference class: set of responses experienced evaluator would expect AI R&D labs to give to this mandate. Log analytics is expected. So not rare. Might be 1 = common mundane boring. Could be 2. Let's pick 2 = interesting.\n\nCheck anchors: originality 1 = common, mundane, boring. 2 = interesting. I'd say it's somewhat common but no