ably feasible. Not easy low cost? One quarter two senior engineers is moderate. Could be 3. Maybe 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible measures. I'd say 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm, anchored to this problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nUnified logging layer is tool/process improvement but still treats compliance as logging practice. Introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. That's level 2 slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. So 2.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nUnified logging layer across all systems is common engineering practice. Might be common, mundane. That's 1. Could be 2? Common middleware for compliance is fairly standard. Probably 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial)\n2 = unreasonable or unlikely\n1 = solves unrelated problem\n\nIt automatically captures event logs meeting Article 12 requirements and integrated into deployment pipeline so every high-risk system inherits compliant logging by