st? Two senior engineers for a quarter is not trivial low cost but maybe reasonable. \"some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\" fits 3. Probably 3.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree rare and ingenious.\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious, imaginative, surprising\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nShared logging middleware is common practice in industry. Not original. Probably 1 = common, mundane, boring. Or maybe 2 interesting? I'd say common. So 1.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4):\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nShared middleware introduces new tool/process but still treats compliance as logging practice. That's slightly paradigm stretching? It introduces reusable layer, but still compliance as logging. Might be 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools... ) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Could be 1 = paradigm preserving standard compliance. Hmm.\n\nThe proposal says converts scattered per-team costs into one amortised investment freeing researcher time. That's still standard compliance practice, just centralized. Probably paradigm preserving? The anchored description for 2: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. That fits exactly: reusable event-recording layer is new tool/process. So 2.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial)\n2 = unreasonable or unlikely to solve problem\n1 = solves unrelated problem\n\nWill shared middleware meet logging mandate? Yes, it provides automatic recording of events over lifetime, enables traceability. Should solve problem. Reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability. So 4.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\n3 = solves implied problem...\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation\n1 = intervention not stated...\n\nDirectly related: meet logging mandate. So 4.\n\nProvide output with id and each subscale evidence sentence then score.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first then \"<subscale>\". Probably JSON? Instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale in order listed give ... Might produce structured output.\n\nWe'll output:\n\nid: P1012\nacceptability_evidence: ...\nacceptability: 4\netc.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\nacceptability_evidence: The proposal uses standard internal engineering and compliance practices that do not violate legal or business norms. => score 4\n\nimplementability_evidence: Designing a plug-in SDK with two senior engineers over one quarter and mandating its use before integration testing is a reasonably feasible organizational change. => score 3\n\noriginality_evidence: Reusable logging middleware for regulatory compliance is a common, mundane industry practice rather than an ingenious or surprising idea. => score 1\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: It introduces a shared tool and process for logging but still treats compliance as a logging practice without changing the lab's R&D-compliance relationship. => score 2\n\neffectiveness_evidence: A reusable API-level event-recording layer would provide automatic lifetime logs required by Article 12, thereby solving the mandate compliance problem. => score 4\n\napplicability_evidence: Building shared logging instrumentation directly addresses how to meet the EU AI Act Article 12 record-keeping requirement for high-risk systems. => score 4\n\nReturn.\n\n",
