al cost could be low.\n\n**Relevance to Article 12 specifically:**\n- This proposal is about CLASSIFICATION (determining whether a system IS high-risk), not about implementing the actual record-keeping/logging requirements of Article 12. It's a prerequisite step — you need to know if Article 12 applies before you implement it. So it's somewhat upstream of the actual technical implementation.\n- From my engineering perspective, this is more of a legal/compliance/process concern than a technical one. The \"compliance checklist\" and \"compliance officer verification\" are organizational mechanisms, not engineering solutions.\n\n**Technical viability assessment:**\n- The proposal as stated is essentially: \"make everyone fill out a form before deploying.\" From an engineering standpoint, this has limited technical content. There's no architecture, no tooling, no automation described. It's a process description.\n- Could be technically viable in the sense that it can be implemented (a checklist exists, someone signs off), but it doesn't offer any technical innovation or engineering value.\n- The risk: if this becomes a bureaucratic bottleneck, it directly impairs prototyping speed and deployment velocity — core concerns of my function.\n\n**My decision:**\nFrom my vantage point (technical viability + speed/quality of prototyping):\n- This is not technically substantive — it's an organizational process proposal.\n- It doesn't propose any engineering solution for logging, record-keeping, or technical implementation.\n- It adds a gate that could slow deployment velocity if not well-designed.\n- However, it's not harmful per se — knowing whether your system is high-risk IS useful information before you invest in building logging infrastructure (which would be expensive to retrofit).\n- But as written, it offers no technical engineering value and primarily addresses legal/compliance risk management, which is another function's domain.\n\nI think this should be rejected from 