ork. My function cares about speed and quality of prototyping — a taskforce that holds \"a series of technical audits\" is the opposite of agile prototyping.\n- However, I should note: without knowing which systems are high-risk, I can't properly design logging architectures into my prototypes from day one. So there IS value in having this clarity early. But does it need to be a \"task force\"? Could this be done more efficiently as a checklist applied at project kickoff?\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom my technical/prototyping vantage point, the underlying goal (knowing which projects need Article 12 logging) is valuable — I want that clarity so I can design logging into architectures from the start rather than retrofitting. However, the proposed mechanism (\"cross-functional review,\" \"series of technical audits,\" dedicated task force) is heavyweight and bureaucratic relative to what's needed. It's not technically viable as a fast-turnaround solution for my prototyping needs — it would likely take weeks or months of meetings before I get clarity. A simpler approach (a classification checklist applied at project intake, with legal sign-off) would serve the same purpose faster.\n\nThe proposal isn't wrong in its intent, but from my specific vantage point of technical viability and prototyping speed/quality, it's an over-engineered governance solution to what is essentially a classification problem. It doesn't produce any engineering artifact I can build on or prototype with. It's a process, not a technical solution.\n\nI'll lean toward reject — not because the goal is wrong, but because the mechanism proposed (a task force holding series of audits) is not technically efficient and would slow down my prototyping work rather than enable it. The promise score should reflect that while I see some value in the underlying need for clarity, the specific proposal as stated doesn't clear my bar for technical viability and prototyping speed.\n\nI'll give it a moderate-low promi