ment for \"automatic recording of events (logs)\" - though this could be seen as adjacent rather than the same thing. Actually, re-reading: Article 12 is about logging capabilities technically built into the system, while Recital 71 discusses both technical documentation AND logs. The proposal addresses the documentation side but not the logging side directly.\n- \"Automatically updated during training and validation phases\" - what about post-deployment changes? The lifetime includes operation. But the proposal says \"throughout its entire lifetime,\" so presumably it covers that too.\n- The scope seems reasonable and well-targeted. It's a documentation infrastructure investment, not an R&D exploration into uncertain territory.\n- From a compliance perspective, this is squarely within the requirement. Not having version-controlled, living technical documentation would be a clear gap in demonstrating compliance with Article 12 and the broader transparency obligations.\n\n**Compliance Officer assessment:**\nThis is a core compliance infrastructure investment. Without it, the lab cannot demonstrate that its technical documentation is \"kept up to date\" as required. It's not optional - it's mandatory for high-risk AI systems. The version control aspect is critical for regulatory inspections and audit trails. The documentation-as-code approach is industry-standard and reduces manual error risk.\n\nHowever, I should note this addresses the documentation side of Article 12/Recital 71 but the proposal doesn't explicitly cover the automatic logging (events) requirement in Article 12(1). That said, the proposal as stated is about technical documentation pipeline specifically, and one could argue the logging capability is a separate engineering task. The proposal still clears my bar because it addresses a mandatory compliance obligation directly and well.\n\nDecision: Approve. This is a clear, targeted response to a specific regulatory requirement. It's not speculative R&D -