        "content": "{\n  \"reasoning\": \"Article 12 is not a paperwork obligation; it imposes a technical design constraint on every high-risk system we ship, and the word 'lifetime' means our logging architecture must survive years of operational use without degradation. For an R&D institution, this forces us to treat event-recording capability as a first-class requirement at the prototype stage rather than a compliance afterthought bolted on before market launch. The specificity of paragraph 3 for Annex III biometric systems is particularly binding: we cannot rely on generic telemetry; we must capture reference databases, matched inputs, and human verifier identities as discrete, retrievable fields. Failure to meet these standards means our systems simply cannot be placed on the EU market, so this is a survival-level constraint, not a nice-to-have. My recommendation is that we treat Article 12 as an architectural input now, before our next development cycle begins.\",\n  \"stance\": 58,\n  \"proposals\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Embed Auto-Logging as Default System Architecture\",\n      \"what\": \"Every new high-risk AI system project must include a purpose-built event-recording layer that captures timestamps, inputs, outputs, and decision triggers automatically from the first prototype cycle.\",\n      \"how\": \"The engineering leads will update our internal design templates to mandate a logging middleware component, and no architecture review is signed off without demonstrating end-to-end log capture over simulated lifetime scenarios.\",\n      \"why\": \"Baking in Article 12 compliance at the architectural level eliminates costly retrofitting and protects us from the technically-allow standard that regulators will test against our deployed systems.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Introduce a Logging Readiness Gate in Our SDLC\",\n      \"what\": \"A formal compliance checkpoint inserted between development and deployment where my office verifies th