gineering where the regulation allows flexibility under Article 12(2).\n\nStance score: The Compliance Officer gave 48. From my resource perspective, I'd say this requires meaningful change but not a complete overhaul. We need to restructure how we allocate engineering time and infrastructure spending, but our core research programs don't fundamentally change - the logging is an add-on layer that changes process more than it changes what we study. However, if our current infrastructure can't support lifetime logging without significant rebuild, that could push higher. I'll go with 52 - slightly above moderate, because the resource reallocation needed (dedicated engineering FTEs for logging infra, ongoing operational costs for log storage and monitoring) is non-trivial but doesn't require abandoning research directions.\n\n(2) Three distinct proposals:\n\n1. A shared logging middleware platform (infrastructure investment) - Build one reusable logging layer that all high-risk model teams integrate with. This is about effective use of existing R&D infrastructure - rather than each team building separate loggers, we invest once in a shared component.\n\n2. A \"logging cost\" line item in project budgeting and funding proposals (resource-allocation) - Restructure how we budget projects so that lifetime logging costs (storage, monitoring, maintenance) are explicitly priced in from the start rather than discovered as overruns. This affects how we apply for grants/funding.\n\n3. A tiered instrumentation protocol based on regulatory specificity (balancing ambition vs. constraints) - For Annex III 1(a) systems where Article 12(3) is prescriptive, full instrumentation is mandatory. For other high-risk categories where Article 12(2) gives more latitude (\"appropriate to the intended purpose\"), we develop a proportional logging framework that avoids over-engineering while meeting minimum requirements. This preserves scientific resources for the actual research rather than compl