        "content": "{\n\"reasoning\": \"From a prototyping-speed standpoint, Article 12(1)'s mandate for automatic lifetime logging is technically straightforward to implement but architecturally constraining: you cannot bolt it on after the inference pipeline is frozen without introducing brittle wrappers that slow every subsequent iteration. The real risk to our development velocity is not the one-time cost of adding a log emitter—it is the per-project tax if each team hand-rolls its own schema, storage, and retention logic for six or seven parallel model variants. The Article 12(3) fields for Annex III 1(a) biometric systems (timestamps, reference database hash, matched inputs, verifier identity) slot cleanly into existing inference wrappers without altering model architecture, so the technical viability is high; what changes is our scaffolding and CI/CD discipline. I also flag that 'lifetime' retention shifts us from ephemeral prototype storage to multi-year persistent event stores, a capacity-planning problem we have not yet scoped into infrastructure budgets.\",\n\"stance\": 54,\n\"proposals\": [\n{\n\"title\": \"Reusable Logging Instrumentation SDK\",\n\"what\": \"A shared Python library that wraps model inference calls to automatically capture the Article 12(3) minimum fields—session start/end timestamps, reference database hash, matched input data, and verifier identity—into a structured event store. Project teams never write logging code by hand again.\",\n\"how\": \"Two senior engineers spend one sprint designing the event schema against 12(3)(a)-(d), then build the SDK with OpenTelemetry-compatible exporters. Every new prototype template in our repository gets a single import line that activates full compliance logging out of the box.\",\n\"why\": \"Converts per-project engineering overhead into a one-time platform investment, protecting iteration speed across all parallel prototyping streams.\"\n},\n{\n\"title\": \"Audit Current Biometric Prototypes for