an system lifetime, including how we handle schema evolution as models are updated (substantial modification tracking per 12(2)(a)). This is an architectural/systems design project. Focus: long-term technical viability and maintainability.\n\nLet me make sure these are genuinely different:\n- #1 is a developer tooling/productivity project (SDK)\n- #2 is a QA/testing infrastructure project (validation harness)\n- #3 is a systems architecture/data engineering project (schema design + retention)\n\nYes, these are distinct: one is about making it fast to add logging, one is about verifying correctness of what's logged, and one is about the long-term data architecture that makes all of this sustainable.\n\nNow let me write each proposal in 60-110 words across four fields (title + what + how + why). Let me count carefully.\n\nLet me draft:\n\n**Proposal 1:**\nTitle: \"Auto-Instrumenting Logging SDK for ML Prototypes\" \nWhat: An internal Python library that wraps our model inference and training pipelines to automatically emit structured, timestamped event logs conforming to Article 12(1)–(3), so engineers ship compliant prototypes without hand-writing logging code. (34 words)\n\nHow: The team builds a decorator-based SDK around our existing inference serving stack; each decorated function captures start/end timestamps, input/output hashes, and model version tags into an append-only event stream. We run it in CI on every prototype merge to verify schema conformance before the artifact is even tagged as \"buildable.\" (47 words)\n\nWhy: It removes per-project logging boilerplate from the critical path of prototyping, directly protecting our iteration speed while making compliance a default rather than an afterthought. \nTotal: 9 + 34 + 47 + 26 = 116... that's over 110. Let me trim.\n\nLet me recount more carefully. The constraint is \"Keep each proposal between 60 and 110 words across its four fields.\" So title + what + how + why combined must be 60-110 words.\n\nLet me r