on sessions against a sandboxed stack; an assertion suite checks the event log for presence, format, and referential integrity of each mandated field. Failures block the release tag in CI. \nWhy: Converts a legal checklist into an executable quality gate so compliance is verified by test rather than audit, keeping prototypes fast and defensible. \nTotal: 7 + 39 + 34 + 26 = 106. Good.\n\n**Proposal 3:**\nTitle: Lifetime Logging Schema and Retention Architecture (7 words)\nWhat: A forward-compatible event-schema design plus object-store retention policy ensuring logs remain queryable and interpretable across the full operational lifetime of a high-risk system, including through model updates that constitute substantial modifications under Article 12(2)(a). \nHow: The data-engineering team defines versioned JSON schemas with semantic-versioning tags, stores immutable log segments in S3-compatible object storage with tiered lifecycle rules (hot for active monitoring, cold for regulatory retention), and writes migration tooling so new schema versions can be applied without dropping historical events. \nWhy: Prevents the technical debt of ad-hoc logging from compounding over years, keeping post-market monitoring viable and protecting the lab's ability to demonstrate compliance long after a system leaves the prototyping phase. \nTotal: 7 + 39 + 46 + 35 = 127... too long. Let me trim.\n\nHow (trimmed): The data team defines versioned JSON schemas with semantic tags, stores immutable segments in object storage with tiered lifecycle rules, and writes migration tooling so new versions apply without dropping historical events. \nWhy: Prevents ad-hoc logging debt from compounding over years, keeping post-market monitoring viable long after a system leaves the prototyping phase. \nTotal: 7 + 39 + 32 + 24 = 102. Good.\n\nLet me also refine my 3-5 sentence reasoning and pick a number.\n\nReasoning (3-5 sentences):\n\"As a team whose core metric is how fast we can go from idea to wo