t workflows and add instrumentation but our core scientific programs don't need redesign.\n\nStance number: I'd say around 35-40. It's not zero because we do need to change how we instrument systems and maintain documentation, but it's far from a major overhaul of what we work on. The science stays; the engineering and process layers shift. Let me go with 38.\n\nNow for 3 distinct proposals:\n\nProposal 1: Retrofitting logging infrastructure into existing R&D pipelines\n- This is about engineering/infrastructure modification\n- Title: \"Retrofit Compliance Logging Into Existing Pipelines\" (7 words) - good\n- What: A targeted project to embed automatic event-recording capabilities into the lab's current model training and inference infrastructure.\n- How: Partner with our DevOps team to instrument existing CI/CD pipelines and deployment environments with standardized log capture modules that satisfy Articles 12(1)-(3).\n- Why: This protects our most valuable asset—existing R&D infrastructure—by making it compliance-ready without rebuilding from scratch, preserving resource-allocation efficiency.\n\nLet me count words for proposal 1:\nTitle: \"Retrofit Compliance Logging Into Existing Pipelines\" \nWhat: A targeted project to embed automatic event-recording capabilities into the lab's current model training and inference infrastructure so that every high-risk system ships with native logging. (29 words)\nHow: Partner with our DevOps team to instrument existing CI/CD pipelines and deployment environments with standardized log-capture modules mapped to Articles 12(1)-(3). (24 words)\nWhy: This protects our most valuable asset—existing R&D infrastructure—by making it compliance-ready without rebuilding, preserving resource-allocation efficiency across funded projects. (22 words)\n\nTotal: 7 + 29 + 24 + 22 = 82 words. Good, within 60-110.\n\nProposal 2: A documentation-as-code / living technical dossier system\n- This is about process/knowledge management\n- Title: \"Liv