n1. Something about integrating logging into existing infrastructure/pipelines (infrastructure-focused)\n2. Something about a specific project or research direction that leverages the logging requirement as an opportunity (research/ambition focused)\n3. Something about resource allocation / funding strategy (funding constraint focused)\n\nLet me think more specifically:\n\nProposal 1: Infrastructure - Build a unified event-logging middleware layer into our existing model deployment pipeline. This is an engineering/infrastructure investment that serves all projects going forward rather than each project building its own logging. Efficient use of existing infrastructure.\n\nProposal 2: Research opportunity - The requirement for biometric systems (Annex III 1(a)) to log reference databases, matched inputs, and verifier identities could be turned into a research advantage. We could develop transparent audit-trail architectures that go beyond compliance minimums, creating a differentiator in public-sector tenders. This turns a cost center into a competitive asset.\n\nProposal 3: Resource allocation - Create a \"compliance budget line\" within each project grant application, and establish a shared logging infrastructure team (2-3 engineers) rather than embedding compliance costs across every research group. This is about efficient resource allocation and protecting scientific ambition from being diluted by scattered compliance overhead.\n\nLet me write these up properly, keeping each proposal between 60-110 words across all four fields combined. Let me count carefully.\n\nLet me draft:\n\n(1) Reasoning (3-5 sentences):\nAs Lab Director, my immediate concern is that Article 12 doesn't change what we research but it does change how we engineer and deploy, and the \"lifetime\" retention requirement converts a one-time build cost into a perpetual operational line item. I want to avoid the trap of scattering compliance overhead across every research group; instead, I'd central