ing our current verification pipeline and adding a signed-event journal plus a compliance dashboard for procurement officers. (32 words)\nWhy: Turning a regulatory floor into a visible trust asset wins contracts where competing labs treat logging as invisible overhead, directly offsetting the compliance cost through revenue. \nTotal: 7+30+32+28 = 97 words. Good.\n\nProposal 3:\nTitle: \"Ring-Fenced Traceability Budget in Every Grant Application\" \nWhat: A standing line item of twelve percent of total engineering effort, labelled \"record-keeping and lifetime traceability,\" inserted into all new project proposals before they reach the funder review stage. (28 words)\nHow: The lab's internal grant-review checklist will now include a mandatory traceability-cost estimate validated by the shared platform team; projects that omit it are flagged back to the PI before submission, and the figure is carried through to post-award budget tracking. (40 words)\nWhy: Naming the cost explicitly prevents silent erosion of scientific scope mid-project and gives funders a transparent rationale for why high-risk AI engineering carries structural overhead beyond raw compute. \nTotal: 7+28+40+31 = 106 words. Good, within range.\n\nLet me double-check word counts more carefully for each proposal.\n\nProposal 1:\nTitle: \"Shared Event-Logging Middleware for All Deployments\" = Shared(1) Event-Logging(2) Middleware(3) for(4) All(5) Deployments(6) = 7 words (counting hyphenated as one? Let me count: Shared, Event-Logging, Middleware, for, All, Deployments = 6 if hyphenated is one word. Actually \"Event-Logging\" could be two. Let me just keep it under 10.)\n\nWhat: \"A single, version-controlled logging layer integrated into our existing model-serving infrastructure that captures the Article 12(2) event categories automatically for every high-risk system we ship, so individual research teams never rebuild traceability from scratch.\"\nLet me count: A(1) single(2) version-controlled(3) logg