        "content": "{\n  \"reasoning\": \"Article 12 imposes a technical architecture obligation—our high-risk systems must be designed to automatically log events over their full lifetime—not merely a documentation duty we discharge after deployment. The 'shall technically allow' language in paragraph 1 means logging capability is a non-negotiable design constraint that must shape our R&D pipelines from the earliest prototyping stage, not something we retrofit before market launch. Paragraph 3's granular requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems (start and end timestamps, reference-database pointers, matched-input records, verifier identities) will directly constrain how we build biometric and identification products in particular. At the same time, this creates a competitive differentiator: if we treat traceability as an engineering strength rather than a regulatory burden, we position ourselves ahead of smaller competitors who will struggle to meet these obligations on short timelines. The lab must integrate compliance engineering into its development lifecycle now, before our first system crosses into high-risk territory and the obligation crystallises.\",\n  \"stance\": 52,\n  \"proposals\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Embedded Traceability Logging Framework\",\n      \"what\": \"A reusable software module built into every high-risk AI system pipeline that automatically captures event logs, timestamps, reference-database pointers, and matched-input records per Article 12(3), functioning as a first-class architectural component from the design phase onward.\",\n      \"how\": \"Engineering will build a standardised logging library with configurable schemas for each Annex III category, integrate it into our CI/CD pipeline so no system ships without passing a traceability test gate, and validate outputs against all four sub-requirements in staging before deployment.\",\n      \"why\": \"Embedding compliance at the architecture level eliminates retrofit risk and ensures every deliverable is audit-ready from day one, protecting institutional survival.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Pre-Market Article 12 Compliance Review Gate\",\n      \"what\": \"A mandatory governance checkpoint between prototype and market-launch phases where a dedicated compliance function verifies that every high-risk system meets all traceability and logging obligations before release. Two trained reviewers staff this gate, reporting directly to this office.\",\n      \"how\": \"We will draft a standardised review checklist mapping each Article 12 paragraph to testable criteria, train two engineers as resident compliance reviewers, and make sign-off at this gate a prerequisite for any production deployment or client handover.\",\n      \"why\": \"A structured internal audit step catches gaps before regulators do, preserving market reputation and preventing costly post-market corrections that could trigger enforcement action against the institution.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Public AI Traceability Specification Project\",\n      \"what\": \"A public-facing project in which the lab publishes an open specification and reference implementation for event-logging in high-risk AI systems, aligned to Article 12 but extending beyond it as best practice.\",\n      \"how\": \"A small team will draft the specification over two quarters, gather feedback from partners and a national surveillance authority, release it under an open licence, and maintain a forum for updates tied to regulatory changes.\",\n      \"why\": \"Publishing a recognised standard amplifies our compliance reputation, gives clients a reason to choose our systems over less transparent competitors, and creates a durable moat around the logging architecture we already embed internally.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
