s an afterthought.\n\n**Number:** I'd say around 45-55. It's a moderate adjustment. We don't need to overhaul what we work on fundamentally—we still develop AI systems—but we do need to restructure how we build them (logging as architecture, not add-on), and if some of our systems fall under Annex III point 1(a) or other high-risk categories, the specific logging requirements will shape product design. It's significant but not existential. I'll go with 52—a moderate, balanced adjustment leaning slightly toward more change because the \"technically allow\" language is forward-looking and affects architecture decisions early in R&D.\n\n**Three distinct proposals:**\n\nThey need to be genuinely different from one another:\n1. Something technical/architectural - building a logging framework into our AI system design\n2. Something process/organizational - establishing a compliance review gate in the R&D pipeline\n3. Something external/market-facing - developing a certification or audit-readiness service, OR perhaps a data governance program for log storage\n\nLet me make them truly distinct:\n1. A technical architecture project: embedding automatic logging capabilities into our AI system frameworks (engineering focus)\n2. An organizational/process change: creating a \"compliance-by-design\" review stage in the R&D lifecycle where Article 12 requirements are validated before systems advance (process/governance focus)\n3. A market/reputation play: developing and publishing an open-source or white-paper contribution on AI system traceability standards that positions us as a thought leader while also codifying our own practices (reputation/market strategy focus)\n\nWait, let me reconsider. The three should be concrete projects or actions the lab could do. Let me refine:\n\n1. Technical: Build an embedded logging module/framework into all high-risk AI system pipelines\n2. Process/organizational: Establish a pre-market compliance audit function specifically for Article 12 trac