        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to act as a Compliance Officer of an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. I need to:\n\n1. Reason in 3-5 sentences about how the lab should respond to Article 12 (Record-keeping).\n2. Give one number from 0-100 for stance on how much to change R&D agenda.\n3. Propose exactly 3 distinct, concrete things the lab could do, each with title, what, how, why fields, keeping each proposal between 60-110 words across its four fields total.\n\nLet me think carefully about this.\n\nMy domain of concern: institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nArticle 12 requires high-risk AI systems to technically allow for automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system. It specifies what logs must capture depending on the category. This is a technical requirement embedded in the system design - it's not just \"keep files\" but build logging capability INTO the architecture.\n\nFor my response reasoning (3-5 sentences):\n- The lab needs to ensure all high-risk AI systems being developed have built-in automatic logging capabilities as a core architectural feature, not an afterthought.\n- This means R&D pipelines need to integrate traceability requirements from design phase through deployment and post-market monitoring.\n- For Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometrics), there are specific minimum data fields that must be logged.\n- The lab's survival depends on compliance; failing to build this in means systems cannot be placed on the market legally.\n- Market reputation: being seen as a compliant, transparent developer is a competitive advantage.\n\nStance number: This is a technical requirement that changes how you build systems but doesn't necessarily change WHAT you build. However, if the lab's R&D agenda includes high-risk AI systems without these capabilities baked in, that's significant. I'd say around 55-65. It's more than moderate because it requires architect