h system's risk-management file.\nWhy: Turning mandatory logging into a visible monitoring service differentiates the lab in procurement conversations and demonstrates ongoing regulatory diligence, strengthening market reputation.\n\nCount: Title(7) + What: A(1) hosted(2) analytics(3) platform(4) that(5) ingests(6) the(7) automatic(8) logs(9) from(10) deployed(11) high-risk(12) systems(13) and(14) produces(15) dashboards(16) covering(17) post-market(18) monitoring(19) under(20) Article(21) 72(22) and(23) operational(24) oversight(25) under(26) Article(27) 26(5)(28), available(29) to(30) both(31) our(32) lab(33) and(34) our(35) enterprise(36) clients(37). = 37 words\n\nHmm that's getting long. Let me trim.\n\nWhat: A hosted platform that ingests automatic logs from deployed high-risk systems and produces dashboards for post-market monitoring under Article 72 and operational oversight under Article 26(5), serving both the lab and enterprise clients.\nCount: A(1) hosted(2) platform(3) that(4) ingests(5) automatic(6) logs(7) from(8) deployed(9) high-risk(10) systems(11) and(12) produces(13) dashboards(14) for(15) post-market(16) monitoring(17) under(18) Article(19) 72(20) and(21) operational(22) oversight(23) under(24) Article(25) 26(5)(26), serving(27) both(28) the(29) lab(30) and(31) enterprise(32) clients(33). = 33 words\n\nHow: Data engineers build a streaming pipeline consuming structured log events; compliance analysts configure threshold alerts tied to risk indicators in each system's risk-management documentation.\nCount: Data(1) engineers(2) build(3) a(4) streaming(5) pipeline(6) consuming(7) structured(8) log(9) events(10); compliance(11) analysts(12) configure(13) threshold(14) alerts(15) tied(16) to(17) risk(18) indicators(19) in(20) each(21) system's(22) risk-management(23) documentation(24). = 24 words\n\nWhy: Converting a compliance obligation into a visible monitoring service differentiates the lab in procurement and demonstrates ongoing regulatory dilig