e scientific roadmap intact while closing the regulatory gap at engineering cost rather than strategic cost.\n\nLet me count words for proposal 1:\nTitle: \"Embed Automatic Logging in Existing System Architectures\" (9 words - but title doesn't count toward the 60-110? Let me re-read... \"Keep each proposal between 60 and 110 words across its four fields.\" So all four fields together must be 60-110 words.)\n\nLet me recount. I need to be more careful. Let me draft and count.\n\nProposal 1:\ntitle: Embed Automatic Logging in Existing System Architectures \nwhat: We audit current high-risk prototypes and retrofit standardized event-logging modules—timestamps, input provenance, database references—directly into the inference pipeline so Article 12(3) minimums are met natively rather than through bolted-on tooling. (35 words)\nhow: Platform engineering runs a two-quarter sprint to build lightweight logging middleware that hooks our existing model-serving stack and writes structured records to the time-series store already in operation for ops monitoring. (34 words)\nwhy: This leverages infrastructure we already fund, avoids new procurement, and closes the compliance gap at engineering cost rather than redirecting scientific ambition. (22 words)\n\nTotal: 9 + 35 + 34 + 22 = 100 words. Good, within 60-110.\n\nProposal 2:\ntitle: Living Technical Documentation Pipeline for System Lifetime \nwhat: We build an automated documentation pipeline that continuously generates and updates the technical file—model characteristics, training data lineage, risk-management records—as each system version ships, satisfying Recital 71's \"kept up to date\" requirement without manual paperwork. (36 words)\nhow: We extend our existing CI/CD pipeline with a documentation-generation stage that pulls metadata from experiment trackers and model registries we already maintain, rendering them into the structured format regulators expect for post-market monitoring. (35 words)\nwhy: This converts a