etly stopped performing as designed.\"\n},\n{\n\"title\": \"Privacy-Preserving Architectures for Biometric Audit Logging\",\n\"what\": \"An applied research project designing cryptographic and data-minimisation techniques that satisfy the specific logging mandates in Article 12(3) — recording match events, reference databases, and verifier identities — while preventing those logs from becoming a secondary surveillance dataset.\",\n\"how\": \"The team would prototype homomorphic or secure-multi-party-computation layers over log stores so that compliance queries can be answered without exposing raw biometric vectors to any single party, including the operator.\",\n\"why\": \"It converts a compliance obligation into an open scientific problem in applied cryptography and data governance, with broader impact on how any regulated system reconciles auditability against re-identification risk.\"\n},\n{\n\"title\": \"Mandated Logs as a Public AI-Behaviour Benchmark\",\n\"what\": \"A data-science initiative to aggregate, de-identify, and structure the lifetime event logs that Article 12 requires high-risk systems to keep into a shared corpus for studying real-world performance decay, edge-case frequency, and cross-system failure patterns.\",\n\"how\": \"We would negotiate with two or three operator partners to contribute anonymised log streams under strict data-use agreements, then build a versioned, schema-standardised repository with public API access and publish baseline drift analyses.\",\n\"why\": \"The resulting corpus addresses a genuine gap in the literature — almost all AI evaluation is pre-deployment — and gives the wider community reproducible evidence on how high-risk systems actually behave once they are live.\"\n}\n]\n}",
