ge its R&D agenda? As a researcher focused on intellectual merit and the knowledge frontier:\n- This isn't a direct research mandate — it's an operational compliance requirement for high-risk systems\n- But it does create new research questions around logging infrastructure, long-term traceability, drift detection, privacy-preserving audit trails\n- It doesn't fundamentally change what we research, but it adds a dimension of \"researching the research infrastructure\" and long-term system behavior\n- I'd say maybe 35-45 — moderate adjustment. We don't overhaul what we work on, but we do need to integrate compliance-aware logging into our research systems and perhaps develop new tools/methods around it. Let me settle on 40.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. My role is specifically about the knowledge frontier, intellectual merit, and broader scientific impact. The regulation doesn't fundamentally change what's scientifically interesting. It adds constraints but also opens questions (long-term drift in logged data, privacy-preserving logging, etc.). I'll go with 35 — a moderate lean toward adjustment without major overhaul.\n\nNow for 3 distinct proposals:\n\n1. Something about developing research tools/methods for long-term AI system monitoring through logs (drift detection over lifetime)\n2. Something about privacy-preserving logging architectures for biometric systems (the Annex III point 1(a) specific requirements raise data protection questions that are scientifically interesting)\n3. Something about using the mandated logging as a scientific dataset — building a public benchmark or methodology for post-market AI system evaluation\n\nLet me make sure these are genuinely different:\n- Proposal 1: Research methods (drift detection, anomaly detection in long temporal logs)\n- Proposal 2: Systems/engineering architecture (privacy-preserving log storage and query for biometric systems)\n- Proposal 3: Data science / benchmarking (leveraging mandated logs as a research corpus for evaluating real-world AI performance degradation)\n\nLet me draft each proposal keeping word count between 60-110 words total across all four fields.\n\nProposal 1: Long-horizon drift detection in operational AI logs\n- title: \"Long-Horizon Drift Detection in Operational AI Logs\" (9 words ✓)\n- what: A research program developing statistical and ML methods to detect capability degradation, distributional shift, or emergent risk signals from the continuous event logs that Article 12 mandates high-risk systems produce over their full lifetime.\n- how: We would build synthetic log generators mimicking multi-year operational timelines, then develop and benchmark change-point detection and anomaly-isolation algorithms against known-injected drift scenarios before validating on anonymized partner-system logs.\n- why: This sits at the intersection of time-series inference and safety science, directly advancing the knowledge frontier on how we know when a deployed AI has quietly stopped performing as designed.\n\nLet me count words: title (9) + what (~35) + how (~30) + why (~28) = ~102 words. Let me recount more carefully.\n\ntitle: \"Long-Horizon Drift Detection in Operational AI Logs\" — 8 words\nwhat: \"A research program developing statistical and ML methods to detect capability degradation, distributional shift, or emergent risk signals from the continuous event logs that Article 12 mandates high-risk systems produce over their full lifetime.\" — let me count: A(1) research(2) program(3) developing(4) statistical(5) and(6) ML(7) methods(8) to(9) detect(10) capability(11) degradation(12) distributional(13) shift(14) or(15) emergent(16) risk(17) signals(18) from(19) the(20) continuous(21) event(22) logs(23) that(24) Article(25) 12(26) mandates(27) high-risk(28) systems(29) produce(30) over(31) their(32) full(33) lifetime(34). = 34 words\nhow: \"We would build synthetic log generators mimicking multi-year operational timelines, then develop and benchmark change