        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to respond as a Development Engineer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is: technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nLet me analyze Article 12 from my perspective as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and prototyping speed/quality.\n\nKey points for my role:\n- Article 12(1) requires \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs)\" - this is an architectural constraint that affects how I design systems\n- Article 12(2) specifies what events need to be logged - risk situations, substantial modifications, post-market monitoring\n- Article 12(3) gives specific minimum fields for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification)\n- From a prototyping speed perspective: adding logging infrastructure can slow down iteration if not designed well\n- From a technical viability perspective: the logging must be automatic, continuous over lifetime, and capture specific data points\n\nMy stance should reflect that as a Dev Engineer, I care about:\n1. How this affects my ability to prototype quickly (logging middleware shouldn't block development)\n2. Technical viability - can we actually build systems that meet these requirements without massive rearchitecture?\n3. Speed/quality tradeoff - adding logging adds overhead but if done well as a shared layer, it's manageable\n\nI think my stance would be around 50-60. It's not zero because the architectural requirement is real and affects how I build things. But it's not a major overhaul because good engineering practice already involves instrumentation, and we can build this as infrastructure rather than changing what we research. The key insight from my vantage: if I design the logging layer as a pluggable middleware that sits between model inference and output, prototyping speed is minimally impacted while compliance is achieved. The risk is treating it as an