ototyping speed—adding compliance instrumentation becomes a one-line import rather than a two-week architectural detour per system.\n\nLet me count: What (30) + How (28) + Why (25) = 83 words for the body, plus title. Let me recount more carefully.\n\nActually let me re-read the constraint: \"Keep each proposal between 60 and 110 words across its four fields.\" So the total of title + what + how + why should be 60-110 words.\n\nLet me recount Proposal 1:\nTitle: \"Shared Logging SDK for All Prototypes\" \nWhat: A reusable middleware library that intercepts model inference calls and emits structured event logs to a central store, so no prototype team reinvents logging plumbing from scratch. (30)\nHow: One two-sprint effort by my team produces the SDK with pluggable backends; each new project links it in sprint one and declares an event schema capped at ten fields. (31)\nWhy: Protects prototyping speed—compliance instrumentation becomes a one-line import rather than a two-week architectural detour per system. (20)\n\nTotal: 7 + 30 + 31 + 20 = 88 words. Good, within range.\n\nProposal 2:\nTitle: \"Event-Schema-First Sprint Zero\" \nWhat: A process change requiring every new high-risk prototype to define its Article 12 event taxonomy and retention policy in a mandatory half-day sprint-zero workshop before any model code is written.\nHow: I build the workshop template and a lightweight validation script that checks whether a proposed schema covers the paragraph-2(a)–(c) categories; project leads sign off before sprints begin.\nWhy: Prevents the most common engineering failure—retrofitting logging after architecture hardens—which costs three to five times more in rework than designing it in.\n\nCount: 5 + 31 + 29 + 28 = 93 words. Good.\n\nProposal 3:\nTitle: \"Sub-Millisecond Logging for Biometric Matching Pipelines\" \nWhat: A focused engineering study building a reference implementation that captures the four Annex III 1(a) minimum fields (use timestamps, database version, matched inputs, verifier ID) with less than one millisecond added latency per inference.\nHow: My team prototypes three architectures—synchronous write-ahead log, async queue with replay, and kernel-level trace hooks—and benchmarks them on our current biometric matching pipeline to find the viable option.\nWhy: If we can hit the latency target, the regulation imposes no measurable performance penalty and we retain full prototyping velocity on time-critical systems.\n\nCount: 7 + 35 + 32 + 28 = 102 words. Good.\n\nNow let me write my 3-5 sentence reasoning as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and prototyping speed/quality:\n\nMy key points:\n- The \"technically allow\" language means this is an architecture-level constraint, not a documentation task\n- From a prototyping speed perspective, the risk is treating logging as a bolt-on after each prototype is built; the solution is to make it part of the base layer so iteration isn't slowed\n- Technical viability: the four minimum fields for Annex III 1(a) are straightforward engineering (timestamps, database versioning, input capture, identity recording) but become tricky under real-time latency constraints in biometric systems\n- The \"lifetime\" requirement means we need storage and log-management infrastructure that doesn't degrade as data grows\n- I'm comfortable at ~58: the change is to how we engineer (add a logging layer, adjust sprint process) not what we research\n\nLet me write this up now.\n",
