test whether the system's logging captures all events relevant for risk identification under Article 79(1), i.e., can we prove our logs would actually flag a risk situation? This is a research/validation activity.\n\nThese are genuinely different: one is an engineering artifact, one is a governance process change, one is a validation/research methodology.\n\nNow let me write each proposal in 60-110 words total across the four fields (title, what, how, why). Plain prose, no bullet lists inside fields. Let me count carefully.\n\nLet me draft:\n\nProposal 1:\n- title: \"Standardized Embedded Logging Framework\" \n- what: A reusable software module that automatically captures and stores event logs across all high-risk AI system prototypes built in the lab, covering start/end timestamps, input data references, and verification personnel identifiers required by Article 12(3). (Let me count... I need to be careful about word counts across all four fields.)\n\nActually, let me re-read: \"Keep each proposal between 60 and 110 words across its four fields.\" So the total of title + what + how + why should be 60-110 words. Let me count carefully.\n\nLet me draft Proposal 1:\ntitle: Standardized Embedded Logging Framework (4 words)\nwhat: A reusable software module that automatically captures and stores event logs across all high-risk AI system prototypes, covering start-end timestamps, reference database identifiers, input data matches, and verification personnel records as mandated by Article 12(3). (37 words)\nhow: The engineering team will integrate this module into the lab's shared container orchestration layer so that every experimental pipeline inherits logging instrumentation by default rather than requiring per-project configuration. (29 words)\nwhy: It eliminates the risk of a system reaching deployment without compliant traceability, protecting us from enforcement action and preserving our market reputation as a responsible developer. (26 words)\n\nTotal: 4 + 37 + 29 + 26 = 96 words. Good, within 60-110.\n\nProposal 2:\ntitle: Mandatory Log Compliance Stage Gate (5 words)\nwhat: A formal review checkpoint inserted into our stage-gate R&D process where the compliance function verifies that each high-risk system's logging architecture satisfies all Article 12(2)(a)-(c) traceability requirements before the project advances to post-market monitoring. (37 words)\nhow: I will draft a standardized checklist mapping each regulatory sub-requirement to observable engineering artefacts, and train project leads to complete it at every gate review; systems failing the check are returned for remediation. (35 words)\nwhy: It shifts compliance from a post-hoc audit burden to a built-in design discipline, reducing last-minute rework and demonstrating to regulators that we operate with genuine systemic oversight. (29 words)\n\nTotal: 5 + 37 + 35 + 29 = 106 words. Good, within 60-110.\n\nProposal 3:\ntitle: Synthetic Risk-Event Logging Validation Harness (6 words)\nwhat: A research evaluation suite that injects synthetic edge-case and risk-triggering inputs into our high-risk systems to verify that the logging layer actually captures events relevant for Article 79(1) risk identification, not merely generic operational data. (35 words)\nhow: The ML validation team will construct adversarial test scenarios calibrated to known failure modes, run them through deployed prototypes in a sandboxed environment, and score whether resulting logs would enable a compliance officer to reconstruct the causal chain of a risk event. (40 words)\nwhy: It gives us empirical evidence that our traceability is functionally sufficient, which strengthens both our post-market monitoring obligations under Article 72 and our defensibility in any regulatory inquiry. (30 words)\n\nTotal: 6 + 35 + 40 + 30 = 111 words. One over. Let me trim.\n\nLet me adjust how: \"The ML validation team will construct adversarial test scenarios calibrated to known failure modes, run them through sandboxed prototypes, and score whether resulting logs enable a compliance officer to reconstruct the causal chain of a risk event.\" (37 words)\n\nTotal: 6 + 35 + 37 + 30 = 108 words. Good.\n\nNow let me write the 3-5 sentence reasoning paragraph and the number.\n\nReasoning (3-5 sentences):\nThe regulation demands that logging be a technical capability of the system itself, not an afterthought or manual process, which means our architecture teams must treat log instrumentation as a first-class design requirement from day one of any high-risk project. For Annex III 1(a) biometric systems specifically, the mandated data fields are precise and non-negotiable, so any lab activity touching that area needs immediate alignment with Article 12(3). The \"lifetime\" qualifier means we cannot treat logging as a deployment-phase concern; it must persist through updates, model retraining cycles, and decommissioning. Our R&D methodology should absorb these constraints without abandoning our research direction, but the engineering scaffolding around every prototype needs to change. I'd rate this at 40 — a meaningful structural adjustment to how we build and validate systems, not a pivot in what we study.\n\nThat's 5 sentences. Good.\n\nNumber: 40.\n\nLet me finalize everything and make sure formatting is clean.\n",
