n to reconstruct system behaviour post-hoc.\",\n      \"why\": \"It converts a regulatory constraint into an intellectual asset that advances interpretability science while simultaneously satisfying Article 12, giving the lab a publication and influence channel competitors lack.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Phased Logging to Protect Exploratory Iteration\",\n      \"what\": \"We adopt a two-tier internal methodology where early-stage prototypes carry lightweight research logs capturing architectural decisions and ablation traces, upgraded to full Article 12(3) compliance only at a formal deployment-readiness gate. This prevents premature engineering lock-in that would stifle rapid hypothesis testing in frontier work.\",\n      \"how\": \"The lab defines a documented science-to-system transition criterion and builds a shared logging SDK so teams can swap instrumentation depth without rewriting experiment code, keeping exploratory iteration cycles under two weeks even for biometric prototypes.\",\n      \"why\": \"It preserves the research velocity and intellectual freedom that make our work meritorious while ensuring no system reaches deployment without full regulatory traceability.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Privacy-Preserving Match Logs for Biometric Verification\",\n      \"what\": \"Article 12(3)(c) requires recording input data that produced a match against the reference database, meaning biometric verification logs store identity-linked features. We study whether cryptographic or statistical techniques such as homomorphic tokens or differential-privacy noising can satisfy traceability without creating a secondary surveillance dataset in our own log infrastructure.\",\n      \"how\": \"The team prototypes match-log pipelines for two candidate architectures, measures fidelity loss to post-market monitoring against privacy risk, and publishes threat models identifying where the mandated data fields create new attack surfaces.\",\n      \"why\": \