      "message": {
        "role": "assistant",
        "content": "{\n  \"reasoning\": \"From a prototyping-velocity standpoint, the operative constraint in Article 12 is that logging must be *technical* and *automatic*, which means structured instrumentation on every inference path becomes a hard architectural dependency rather than an optional telemetry add-on. The real speed risk to us is not the scientific content of the regulation but the coupling it introduces: if each new biometric architecture or verification pipeline requires bespoke log-capture code, our iterate-test-deploy cycle slows by one to two sprints per prototype, and that compounds across a portfolio. The 'lifetime' qualifier adds a second engineering burden I had not previously budgeted—logs from checkpoint v1 must remain schema-compatible and queryable after we have swapped in model v7, which demands an explicit versioning strategy baked into the data layer now, before architectures calcify. For Annex III 1(a) systems specifically, the mandated fields (matched input data, reference database identifiers, verifier identity) route sensitive PII through our logging pipeline, so encryption and access control must be designed into the instrumentation SDK from day one rather than patched after a security review flags it. The practical response is to treat compliance logging as shared infrastructure with a fixed interface, so that each new prototype plugs in an adapter rather than reinventing capture logic, keeping per-prototype overhead near zero while satisfying the letter of the regulation.\",\n  \"stance\": 45,\n  \"proposals\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Standard Log-Instrumentation SDK for High-Risk Prototypes\",\n      \"what\": \"A shared middleware library that automatically captures Article 12(3) mandated fields—session start and end timestamps, reference database identifiers, matched input data, and verifier identity—for any high-risk system prototype without requiring per-project logging engineering.\",\n      \"how\": \"The prototypin