        "content": "{\n  \"reasoning\": \"From my vantage point as someone who cares about whether prototypes actually work and ship fast: Article 12 does not change what AI systems we can build, but it changes how we instrument every prototype from day one. The lifetime-logging requirement means our current practice of bolting observability onto late-stage demos is no longer viable; we need structured, schema-compliant event capture baked into the inference pipeline during design, not retrofitted before a compliance review. For Annex III 1(a) biometric work specifically, the four mandated data fields (use timestamps, reference-database ID, matched input data, verifier identity) are straightforward to emit but force us to rethink storage durability and access control so logs remain queryable across multi-year deployment windows without becoming a throughput bottleneck on high-frequency matching loops. The practical response is to treat logging as a first-class engineering deliverable inside every prototype sprint rather than a compliance afterthought handed off to legal weeks before a product hand-off.\",\n  \"stance\": 45,\n  \"proposals\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Compliance Logging Middleware for Prototypes\",\n      \"what\": \"A shared, embeddable logging layer that automatically captures the event fields mandated by Article 12(2)-(3)—risk-relevant triggers, post-market monitoring signals, and biometric-specific records—into a structured, queryable store.\",\n      \"how\": \"We build it as a thin SDK in our primary inference stack with configurable schemas per Annex III category, so any new prototype inherits compliant logging without custom integration work from the first sprint iteration.\",\n      \"why\": \"It removes the largest recurring engineering cost of retrofitting logs into late-stage prototypes and directly accelerates time-to-viable-demonstration across every project in the lab.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Logging Completeness Gate in the Prototype Cycle\",\n      \"what\": \"A mandatory review checkpoint at design-freeze and integration-complete where a designated engineer confirms the prototype emits every log category its risk classification requires before it advances to the next stage.\",\n      \"how\": \"We map a short structured checklist directly onto Article 12(2)(a)-(c) and, for biometric systems, 12(3)(a)-(d), then embed it as a blocking item in our sprint board backed by automated schema-validation scripts that parse emitted logs at runtime.\",\n      \"why\": \"It surfaces missing log fields while they are cheap to add in early prototypes instead of discovering gaps during a compliance audit months downstream.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Low-Overhead Logging for High-Frequency Inference\",\n      \"what\": \"A focused research spike to benchmark whether the mandatory per-use logging of Article 12(3)—timestamps, reference-database IDs, matched inputs, verifier identities—degrades throughput or latency in high-frequency biometric matching pipelines.\",\n      \"how\": \"We build a minimal prototype loop for face and fingerprint matching at target rates, instrument it with full Article 12(3) logging, and measure the CPU, memory, and end-to-end-latency cost of each mandatory field to identify which are cheap versus expensive.\",\n      \"why\": \"If unaddressed, compliance logging could push our best-performing models below viability thresholds; quantifying the overhead now lets us budget engineering time before a product team inherits the problem.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
