ntact, and makes lifetime automatic recording an architectural default rather than a retrofit that stalls releases.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Living Tech-Docs Pipeline Tied to CI/CD\",\n      \"what\": \"A documentation-as-code workflow where the Recital 71 technical documentation—algorithms, data, training, testing, validation processes, risk-management system—is generated and versioned from the same codebase as the model itself.\",\n      \"how\": \"Extend our CI pipeline so every merge or release triggers regeneration of compliance-relevant doc sections; flag stale documents when upstream code changes but docs haven't been regenerated.\",\n      \"why\": \"Makes 'kept up to date' a mechanical guarantee rather than a manual chore, and gives us one source of truth for both engineering review and regulatory inspection.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Log-Fidelity Benchmark for Prototyping-to-Production Handoff\",\n      \"what\": \"A test harness that quantifies whether a prototype's logging captures all Article 12(2)(a)-(c) event types and Annex III 1(a) minimums at the same fidelity as production, catching gaps before they become compliance failures.\",\n      \"how\": \"Define a reference log-schema per system class; run automated conformance checks in staging comparing prototype logs against the schema each sprint; track pass-rate metrics per project.\",\n      \"why\": \"Gives us an objective engineering metric for whether a system technically allows automatic recording, so viability risks surface early rather than at integration or audit.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to respond as a Development Engineer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is: technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nLet me analyze the regulation from my vantage point:\n\nArticle 12 requires:\n- Automatic recording of events (logs) over lifetime of system (Art 12(1))\n- Logging must enable traceability for risk identification, post-market monitoring, operation monitoring (Art 12(2))\n- For Annex III 1(a) systems: minimum logging of use periods, reference databases, matched input data, verifier identities (Art 12(3))\n\nRecital 71 adds: technical documentation must be kept up to date, algorithms/data/training/testing/validation processes documented, risk-management system documented.\n\nFrom a Development Engineer perspective focused on technical viability and prototyping speed/quality:\n\nKey concerns:\n- Logging infrastructure is a structural requirement that affects every prototype we build - it can't be bolted on later\n- The \"automatic recording over lifetime\" means our systems need persistent storage, structured logging pipelines, retention policies designed in from day one\n- For Annex III 1(a) systems (identity verification), the specific minimums (timestamps, reference DB provenance, matched inputs, verifier IDs) are very concrete engineering specs\n- Recital 71's \"up to date\" documentation means our tech docs aren't static deliverables but living artifacts - this affects how we structure code repos, CI/CD pipelines, versioning\n- Speed of prototyping: if every prototype needs logging infrastructure from the start, it adds overhead. But if we build reusable logging frameworks/middleware, the marginal cost drops fast.\n- Technical viability: \"automatic recording\" means no human-in-the-loop for log generation - the system must self-document its events. This is an architectural constraint.\n\nMy stance: I'd say around 52-55. It's not a full overhaul (we're not changing what we build), but it does require structural changes to how we prototype and deploy. We need logging middleware, documentation-as-code practices, and specific data-capture modules for Annex III 1(a) work. The prototyping speed impact is real but manageable if we invest in shared infrastructure early.\n\nLet me craft my response:\n\n(1) Reasoning (3-5 sentences):\n- From a technical viability standpoint, Article 12(1)'s \"technically allow for automatic recording\" is an architectural constraint that must be embedded in our system designs before the first prototype iteration, not retrofitted. The Annex III 1(a) minimums in paragraph 3 are essentially a data-schema specification (timestamps, DB provenance, matched inputs, verifier IDs) that maps cleanly to structured event logging and can be handled with a shared middleware layer without slowing individual prototyping cycles. Recital 71's \"up to date\" documentation mandate means our tech docs need to live in the same repos and CI pipelines as code, which is actually compatible with how we already version experiments but requires a small process shift. The speed-of-prototyping risk is real if every team independently builds logging from scratch, so the engineering answer is a reusable logging-and-traceability framework that gets integrated early, making the marginal cost per project low. Net effect: moderate structural adjustment to our build pipeline and documentation practices, not a change in what problems we solve.\n\nStance: 53/100\n\nNow three distinct proposals:\n\n1. A shared logging/traceability middleware layer (infrastructure play)\n- Title: \"Reusable Event-Logging Middleware for All Prototypes\"\n- What: A shared library/framework that auto-captures the Article 12(2)(a)-(c) event types and the Annex III 1(a) minimums as structured, timestamped events with configurable retention.\n- How: Build it as a thin instrumentation layer in our SDK that every protot