e logging schema alongside model code so logs are reproducible per release and update automatically as the system evolves.\" Build(1) it(2) into(3) our(4) existing(5) prototyping(6) SDK(7) so(8) every(9) new(10) system(11) links(12) it(13) at(14) integration(15); version-control(16) the(17) logging(18) schema(19) alongside(20) model(21) code(22) so(23) logs(24) are(25) reproducible(26) per(27) release(28) and(29) update(30) automatically(31) as(32) the(33) system(34) evolves(35). = 35 words\n- Why (1 sentence): \"Removes per-project reimplementation overhead, keeps our iteration velocity intact, and makes lifetime automatic recording an architectural default rather than a retrofit that stalls releases.\" Removes(1) per-project(2) reimplementation(3) overhead(4), keeps(5) our(6) iteration(7) velocity(8) intact(9), and(10) makes(11) lifetime(12) automatic(13) recording(14) an(15) architectural(16) default(17) rather(18) than(19) a(20) retrofit(21) that(22) stalls(23) releases(24). = 24 words\n\nTotal: 4+30+35+24 = 93 words. Good, within 60-110.\n\n2. Documentation-as-code pipeline (process/tooling play)\n- Title: \"Living Tech-Docs Pipeline Tied to CI/CD\"\n- What: A documentation-as-code workflow where the Recital 71 technical documentation (algorithms, data, training/testing/validation processes, risk-management system) is generated and versioned from the same codebase as the model itself.\n- How: Extend our CI pipeline so that every merge or release triggers regeneration of the compliance-relevant doc sections; flag stale documents when upstream code changes but docs haven't been regenerated.\n- Why: Makes \"kept up to date\" a mechanical guarantee rather than a manual chore, and gives us a single source of truth that satisfies both engineering review and regulatory inspection without duplicating effort.\n\nWord count:\nTitle: Living Tech-Docs Pipeline Tied to CI/CD (8 words)\nWhat: A documentation-as-code workflow where the Recital 71 technical documentation—algor