        "role": "assistant",
        "content": "{\n  \"reasoning\": \"The science we pursue does not change under Article 12; what changes is the engineering cost structure around it. The 'lifetime' logging mandate means recurring storage and maintenance expenditure on every high-risk system, which hits our multi-year budget planning hard if we treat each project's logging as a bespoke afterthought rather than a shared infrastructure component we already partially operate through our monitoring dashboards. My concern is that without deliberate resource reallocation now, compliance engineering will silently cannibalise the headcount and compute we have earmarked for model-development milestones over the next three fiscal years, forcing us to either slow scientific delivery or accept a growing technical-debt backlog on traceability. The efficient path is to build one logging backbone, embed it in our deployment pipeline as a standard gate, and ring-fence its budget explicitly so that the trade-off against speculative research lines is visible to the board rather than absorbed invisibly. I am not changing which problems we attack; I am restructuring how engineering hours and storage dollars flow through them.\",\n  \"stance\": 47,\n  \"proposals\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Shared Logging Backbone for High-Risk Projects\",\n      \"what\": \"A centralized logging middleware layer that every high-risk AI system integrates at build time, providing automatic event capture, lifetime storage, and structured retrieval in one shared service rather than per-project bespoke solutions.\",\n      \"how\": \"We extend our existing monitoring stack—already running model-performance dashboards—with the Article 12(2) event schemas, deploy it as a lab-wide service, and assign two engineers to an eighteen-month maintenance cycle.\",\n      \"why\": \"Amortising one engineering investment across all Annex III projects cuts per-system compliance overhead by roughly forty percent and eliminates duplicative rework