ctually INCREASES speed because you're not retrofitting. But it does add constraints to architecture decisions. I'd say maybe 30-40 range - moderate impact on build process, minimal impact on what we research/build.\n\nLet me settle on a stance of around 32. The science/research agenda is unchanged, but the engineering scaffolding for prototyping needs to incorporate logging as a first-class concern from the start. It's not a major overhaul (not 50+) because it's an infrastructure layer change, not a \"stop doing X and do Y instead\" change.\n\nNow for 3 distinct concrete proposals:\n\n1. **Compliance-aware prototype scaffolding / SDK** - Build a shared logging middleware library that every prototype imports from day one. This is about the dev tooling I use daily to spin up prototypes quickly while being compliant by default. Focus on prototyping speed.\n\n2. **Log schema design and validation pipeline in CI/CD** - Create structured event schemas (JSON Schema or Protocol Buffers) for Article 12 events, with automated validation gates in our build pipelines so that any prototype that doesn't emit correct log structures fails the build. This is about technical viability - ensuring the \"automatic recording\" actually works correctly.\n\n3. **Lifetime logging storage architecture prototype** - A proof-of-concept demonstrating how to architect storage for lifetime retention of Annex III 1(a) data (matched inputs, reference DB versions, timestamps, verifier IDs) with appropriate access controls, so we can validate technical feasibility and cost before committing product lines into that category. This is about validating that the requirement is technically achievable within our constraints.\n\nLet me make sure these are genuinely different:\n- #1 is about developer tooling / code scaffolding (how I write code for prototypes)\n- #2 is about CI/CD pipeline integration and schema validation (how we verify compliance in builds)\n- #3 is about backend storage architecture (how