        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to respond as a Compliance Officer of an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act, specifically Article 12 on Record-keeping. Let me break down what's needed:\n\n1. Reason in 3-5 sentences about how the lab should respond to this regulation.\n2. Give one number from 0 to 100 for stance on how much the R&D agenda should change.\n3. Propose exactly 3 distinct, concrete things the lab could do. Each with title (≤10 words), what (1-2 sentences), how (1-2 sentences), why (1 sentence). Each proposal between 60-110 words total across four fields.\n\nLet me think about this from a Compliance Officer's perspective: institutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation.\n\nArticle 12 requires:\n- Automatic logging over the system's lifetime\n- Logging must capture events relevant to risk identification (Art 79(1)), substantial modifications, post-market monitoring (Art 72), and operation monitoring (Art 26(5))\n- For biometric systems (Annex III point 1(a)), minimum requirements include: recording of each use period, reference database used, input data for matches, identification of natural persons in verification\n\nAs a Compliance Officer, I need to think about:\n- Whether the lab's systems are high-risk or not (if they're biometric, the Annex III requirements kick in)\n- The lifetime nature of logging - this is a persistent requirement\n- Technical architecture changes needed for automatic event recording\n- The \"substantial modification\" trigger for logging\n- Post-market monitoring integration\n\nLet me think about the stance number. Article 12 is about record-keeping and logging. It's an operational/technical requirement rather than something that fundamentally changes what you build. However, it does require:\n- Technical architecture to support automatic logging throughout lifetime\n- Logging capabilities designed from the start (not bolted on)\n- For biometric systems specifically, ver