otal: 93 words. Good.\n\nProposal 2: \"Pre-Design Logging Impact Gate in Project Intake\"\n- what: A new checkpoint inserted between project proposal and funded start, where the lead engineer must submit a one-page logging architecture plan specifying event types, retention period, storage location, and the modification-trigger definitions under Article 12(2)(a). (36 words)\n- how: The gate is enforced by a lightweight review panel of two (one senior engineer, one compliance liaison) that meets biweekly; proposals without the plan are returned to the proponent within five working days. (30 words)\n- why: It front-loads the cost of regulatory thinking into the budgeting stage rather than discovering mid-development that logging was never scoped, avoiding costly rework and protecting already-tight project timelines. (30 words)\nTotal: 96 words. Good.\n\nProposal 3: \"Efficient Lifetime Log Retention as a Research Problem\"\n- what: A small funded research thread exploring adaptive log-compression, tiered storage, and retention-policy optimisation so that the \"lifetime of the system\" obligation does not become an unbounded operational expense for the lab. (29 words)\n- how: We assign one postdoc or senior researcher six months of protected time to benchmark compression ratios, query-latency trade-offs, and cost-per-gigabyte across cloud tiers, producing a reference architecture document our engineering teams adopt. (31 words)\n- why: It turns an open-ended regulatory cost into a bounded technical problem we can solve with existing staff, keeping operating budgets predictable while still meeting the Article 12(1) lifetime requirement in good faith. (30 words)\nTotal: 90 words. Good.\n\nNow let me write the full response in my Lab Director voice, focused on resource allocation, funding constraints, and infrastructure use.\n",
