able results with direct regulatory relevance.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Benchmarking Traceability Quality Across Logging Schemes\",\n      \"what\": \"We would develop a standardised evaluation suite that scores logging implementations against the traceability criteria in Article 12(2), treating log utility as a measurable scientific quantity with defined axes such as event coverage, temporal resolution, and reconstruction fidelity.\",\n      \"how\": \"The project would construct synthetic incident scenarios drawn from Annex III use cases, run them through candidate logging configurations, and quantify how well each configuration supports downstream risk identification under Article 79(1) and post-market monitoring tasks.\",\n      \"why\": \"A public benchmark elevates the intellectual rigour of a currently engineering-driven area and gives the broader community a shared vocabulary for comparing approaches.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Open Log Corpus for Post-Market Monitoring Research\",\n      \"what\": \"We would curate and release a large-scale synthetic log corpus that mimics the event streams required under Article 12(2)(b), annotated with injected faults, drift episodes, and risk indicators. This gives researchers outside our lab a reproducible substrate for studying detection algorithms.\",\n      \"how\": \"Generation scripts would simulate the operational profiles of several Annex III categories over multi-year timescales; annotation pipelines would label anomaly windows using ground-truth injection metadata rather than post-hoc heuristics, ensuring reproducibility across independent labs.\",\n      \"why\": \"An open dataset amplifies broader scientific impact far beyond our own publications and establishes the lab as a reference point in the emerging field of regulatory-compliant AI operations.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
