        "content": "{\n\"reasoning\": \"From my perspective at the knowledge frontier, Article 12 is simultaneously a constraint on experimental flexibility and an unprecedented opportunity to build longitudinal research data on AI system behaviour in deployment — something the current literature almost entirely lacks. The mandated lifetime logs create a continuous observation stream that, if designed thoughtfully from day one, lets us study drift, edge-case accumulation, and performance decay with a temporal resolution no controlled experiment can match. However, the GDPR friction around named verifiers and reference-database inputs means we must architect data-protection boundaries before locking in logging schemas, or we will lose secondary research access to our own production telemetry. I see this as a methodology shift rather than an agenda overhaul: what we study stays largely the same, but how we instrument, document, and interpret our systems must change so that scientific rigour and regulatory traceability emerge from the same design decisions.\",\n\"stance\": 48,\n\"proposals\": [\n{\n\"title\": \"Production-Log Drift Observatory\",\n\"what\": \"A dedicated research node ingesting Article-12-compliant logs from all deployed high-risk systems to detect performance drift, distribution shift, and edge-case accumulation via longitudinal statistical methods over months and years of operation.\",\n\"how\": \"We build a shared ingestion layer aligned with the Lab Director's infrastructure plan that normalises log schemas across projects, then apply time-series anomaly detection and causal-inference techniques to quantify when and why system behaviour diverges from training-distribution expectations.\",\n\"why\": \"This yields a first-of-its-kind longitudinal dataset on deployed AI performance, giving our lab a unique citation advantage in a gap the current literature cannot fill through short-horizon benchmarks alone.\"\n},\n{\n\"title\": \"Traceability-First Experimental Design Protocol\",\n\"what\": \"A lab-wide methodological standard requiring every new high-risk experiment to be designed so its native outputs constitute a valid Article 12 log stream, making compliance an inherent by-product of rigorous instrumentation rather than a post-hoc bolt-on.\",\n\"how\": \"We codify a checklist covering timestamped input-output pairs, reference-database identifiers, and verifier metadata fields into existing experiment templates and peer-review gates, so the data architecture chosen for publication-quality reproducibility automatically satisfies regulatory record-keeping.\",\n\"why\": \"This eliminates a redundant documentation pass, protects researcher time, and raises the baseline intellectual merit of every paper we publish by embedding full traceability as default methodology rather than an optional appendix.\"\n},\n{\n\"title\": \"Open AI Performance Transparency Papers\",\n\"what\": \"A publication series drawing on Recital 71-mandated technical documentation — capabilities, limitations, training and validation processes — to produce peer-reviewed papers analysing how specific architectural choices affect real-world system behaviour over the deployment lifetime.\",\n\"how\": \"We select two or three deployed systems each year whose accumulated logs reach statistical maturity, anonymise per GDPR constraints flagged by Compliance, and publish comparative analyses in top-tier venues linking design decisions to observable performance trajectories.\",\n\"why\": \"This converts a compliance obligation into high-impact scientific output advancing the field's understanding of long-horizon AI behaviour while positioning our lab as a reference point for evidence-based governance research.\"\n}\n]\n}",
