st an Article 12 schema, verifying field presence, timestamp continuity, and Annex III 1(a) specific data elements before allowing merge or deployment. (35 words)\n- how: We write a set of schema validators and temporal-integrity checkers in Python/TypeScript that run on every pull request touching high-risk system code; failures block the merge with a human-readable diff showing which mandated fields are missing. (36 words)\n- why: Converts a compliance requirement from a manual review burden into an automated quality signal, catching logging gaps while they are still one-line fixes rather than post-deployment retrofits that stall delivery. \nTotal: 7+35+36+29 = 107 words. Good, under 110.\n\nNow let me write the reasoning (3-5 sentences) and stance score.\n\nReasoning as Dev Engineer focused on technical viability and prototyping speed/quality:\n- Article 12 is an architectural constraint that hits at the scaffolding level - if not designed in from prototype phase, retrofit costs are severe\n- The \"automatic\" requirement means logging must be code-level, not process-level; it changes our inference pipeline architecture\n- For prototyping speed specifically, building this into shared infrastructure (SDK + templates) actually accelerates work long-term by removing per-project re-invention\n- The Annex III 1(a) specifics create real schema design questions we need to resolve before locking in data pipelines\n- \"Lifetime\" durability means our logging stores and schemas must be versioned and migration-safe, not throwaway debug artifacts\n\nStance: I'll go with 53. It's a meaningful shift in HOW we build (architecture, scaffolding, CI) but doesn't change WHAT we research or the fundamental nature of our AI systems. Moderate adjustment to engineering practice, not an agenda overhaul.\n",
