pliance into our architecture, train engineers on new requirements, but the fundamental scientific questions we pursue don't change. Maybe 25 or 30. Let me go with 28.\n\nNow for 3 genuinely distinct proposals:\n\n1. Something about integrating logging infrastructure into existing development pipelines (infrastructure/engineering focus)\n2. Something about a specific research project to develop efficient logging architectures that minimize performance overhead (scientific/R&D angle - this could even become publishable work)\n3. Something about governance/process - allocating funding and resources, training staff, updating SDLC\n\nLet me make them genuinely different:\n- Proposal 1: Technical/engineering - retrofitting/logging architecture integration into our existing codebase pipeline\n- Proposal 2: Research-oriented - developing lightweight, efficient logging mechanisms that don't degrade system performance (this is a genuine R&D contribution)\n- Proposal 3: Organizational/funding - restructured sprint planning and budget allocation to embed compliance as a cross-cutting requirement rather than a separate project\n\nLet me draft these and count words carefully. Each proposal needs 60-110 words total across title, what, how, why.\n\nProposal 1:\nTitle: \"Integrate Audit Logging into Existing CI/CD Pipeline\" \nWhat: We embed structured event-logging modules directly into the continuous integration and deployment pipeline that already ships our high-risk prototypes to test environments, so every build automatically captures the Article 12(3) fields—usage timestamps, reference databases, input data, and verifier identities. (38 words... let me count more carefully)\n\nLet me recount:\nTitle: \"Integrate Audit Logging into Existing CI/CD Pipeline\" - 9 words\nWhat: \"We embed structured event-logging modules directly into the continuous integration and deployment pipeline that already ships our high-risk prototypes to test environments, so every build automatically ca