\"why\": \"Converts a per-project logging tax into a single reusable component, preserving prototyping velocity while making compliance structural.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Log-Schema Drift Detection in CI Gates\",\n      \"what\": \"A continuous-integration check that validates every model retraining or architectural commit against the declared logging schema, flagging changes that would break traceability over the system's lifetime.\",\n      \"how\": \"We extend our existing pipeline with a schema-contract test that parses the diff, maps touched modules to their logged fields, and fails the build if coverage drops below the Article 12 minimums.\",\n      \"why\": \"Protects the lifetime requirement in practice by catching silent regressions at commit time rather than during an audit years later.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Prototype-to-Production Logging Sufficiency Gate\",\n      \"what\": \"A staging validation environment where any prototype nominated for high-risk deployment must pass an automated test confirming its logs satisfy Article 12(2)(a) through (c) before promotion to production infrastructure.\",\n      \"how\": \"We build a replay harness that feeds synthetic and historical inputs through the candidate system, then verifies each required event category was captured with correct schema and timestamping.\",\n      \"why\": \"Gives development teams an objective pass-fail criterion at the prototyping-to-deployment boundary so speed is not sacrificed to guesswork about what sufficient logging means in practice.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to respond as a Development Engineer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nLet me think about this from my specific vantage point: I care about whether things actually work technically, how fast we can prototype, and quality of the engineering output. The Compliance Officer already covered the \"we need to build it in\" angle. The Lab Director covered resource allocation. The Researcher found the interpretability angle interesting.\n\nFrom a Development Engineer perspective, my concerns are:\n- Can we actually implement compliant logging without killing our prototyping speed?\n- What's the technical architecture that makes this viable across diverse model types (the researcher mentioned transformers vs RL agents)?\n- How do we make logging modular/pluggable so it doesn't slow down iteration cycles?\n- The \"lifetime\" requirement is technically challenging - how do you maintain log schemas across years of system evolution, model retraining, etc.?\n- The minimum fields in Article 12(3) for Annex III systems are actually quite specific and bounded - that's good news for engineering.\n\nMy stance: I'd say around 50-60. Not a major overhaul (the core R&D questions don't change), but it does require concrete architectural changes to how we build prototypes. The key tension is speed of prototyping vs. the requirement that logging be \"technical\" and automatic from the start. If we bolt it on later, we've failed Article 12(1). So our prototyping methodology needs a shift - not in what we build but in how we scaffold it.\n\nLet me settle on 58/100. It's moderate - we need to change how we architect and gate prototypes, but not what science we pursue. The engineering tax is real but bounded for Annex III systems with their specific minimum fields.\n\nNow for 3 distinct proposals:\n\n1. A logging abstraction layer / SDK that sits in the prototyping pipeline - this is about making compliance a build-time concern, not an afterthought. It's an infrastructure/engineering project.\n\n2. A \"log schema design\" process integrated into the architecture review phase - this is about governance of what gets logged and ensuring the technical documentation (Recital 71) stays current. This is more of a process/methodology change.\n\n3. A prototype-to-production logging migration harness - addressing the speed/quality concern specifically. How do you take a fast-moving research prototype and transition it to a production system without breaking the logging contract? This addresses my core concern about prototyping velocity vs. compliance.\n\nWait, let me make sure these are genuinely different:\n- Proposal 1: Build an engineering tool (SDK/library) - concrete artifact\n- Proposal 2: A technical documentation auto-generation pipeline tied to code changes - this is about keeping the Recital 71 docs current automatically\n- Proposal 3: A staging environment / test harness that validates logging compliance before a system exits the prototype phase\n\nLet me refine:\n\n1. **Composable Logging SDK for Prototype Pipelines** - An internal library/framework that provides drop-in logging instrumentation for common model architectures (transformers, RL agents, pipelines). Engineers import it at scaffold time and get Article 12(3) fields captured automatically. This is a BUILD project.\n\n2. **Automated Log-Schema Drift Detection in CI/CD** - A CI gate that validates every code change against the declared log schema, flagging when model retraining or architectural changes would break the logging contract over the system's lifetime. This is an OPERATIONAL/QUALITY project addressing the \"lifetime\" requirement.\n\n3. **Prototype-to-Production Logging Contract Validation Harness** - A staging test environment where any prototype that might become a high-risk system must pass an automated \"logging sufficiency\" check before it can be promoted to production. This gates on speed vs. compliance explicitly.\n\nThese are distinct: (1) is about building the tool, (2) is about maintaining the contract over time via CI, (3) is about the gate/transition point between prototyping and deployment.\n\nLet me write this up now in plain prose, keeping each proposal between 60-110 words across all four fields combined.\n\nLet me count words carefully for each proposal:\n\nProposal 1:\n- title: \"Composable Logging SDK for Prototype Scaffolding\" (7 words - good)\n- what: A shared internal library that provides drop-in instrumentation modules for our most common architecture families—transformers, RL agents, and hybrid pipelines—capturing the Article 12(3) minimum fields automatically at inference time. Engineers import one module when scaffolding a new prototype and get compliant event logging without hand-coding it per project.\n- how: A small platform team (two engineers, three months) builds the SDK with adapters for PyTorch, JAX, and our in-house orchestration layer, then integrates it into the standard project template so every new repo starts with a logging contract already wired.\n- why: It converts a per-project engineering tax into a one-time library investment, keeping prototyping velocity intact while making Article 12(1) compliance structural rather than aspirational.\n\nLet me count: title (7) + what (~65) + how (~40) + why (~30) = ~142. Too long. Need to trim to 60-110 total.\n\nLet me recount more carefully. The constraint is \"between 60 and 110 words across its four fields.\" So title + what + how + why combined should be 60-110 words. That's tight!\n\nProposal 1:\n- title: Composable Logging SDK for Prototype Scaffolding \n- what: A shared internal library providing drop-in instrumentation that captures Article 12(3) fields automatically at inference time, imported once when scaffolding any new prototype. \n- how: Two platform engineers build adapters