ording in system architecture\",\n      \"what\": \"A redesign of our reference architecture templates so that every new high-risk system design includes a dedicated event-recording subsystem covering log schema, retention policy, and tamper-evidence as a first-class component before any code is written.\",\n      \"how\": \"I lead a two-week sprint where we refactor our five active project architectures to embed the Article 12(2)(a) through (c) event categories into their design documents, then codify the pattern in an internal architecture decision record that all new prototypes must reference during design review.\",\n      \"why\": \"Catching logging gaps at the whiteboard stage is far cheaper than retrofitting them after a prototype has matured, protecting our prototyping speed where it matters most.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Automated runtime log-compliance verifier\",\n      \"what\": \"A CI/CD gate tool that after each high-risk model deployment injects synthetic inputs and verifies the system's logs actually capture all Article 12(3) fields before marking the build as releasable.\",\n      \"how\": \"I prototype it in two sprints on our staging environment; the tool parses structured log output against a JSON-Schema derived from the regulation and fails the pipeline on any missing or malformed field, feeding results into our post-market monitoring dashboard under Article 72.\",\n      \"why\": \"It converts a manual compliance review into an automated quality gate so engineers get immediate pass-or-fail feedback on logging without adding weeks of overhead per prototype.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
