        "content": "{\n\"reasoning\": \"Article 12 is not a documentation formality; it is a technical design mandate stating that high-risk systems shall technically allow automatic recording of events over their entire lifetime. If any system we ship qualifies as high-risk—particularly under Annex III point (a) on biometric identification—the absence of compliant logging means we cannot lawfully place that system on the EU market, which is an existential threat to institutional survival. The recital makes clear this spans the full operational life of each product, so logs are not a development-phase artefact but a permanent infrastructure obligation with retention and retrieval implications for potentially decades. I therefore view this as forcing us to treat logging as a first-class engineering concern at the architecture stage rather than bolting it on after deployment. The immediate practical step is to audit every current and planned pipeline against the minimum fields in Article 12(3), because retrofitting into already-deployed systems is technically harder, slower, and reputationally damaging if a market-surveillance authority discovers the gap.\",\n\"stance\": 60,\n\"proposals\": [\n{\n\"title\": \"Embed Logging Architecture into All High-Risk Pipelines\",\n\"what\": \"A mandatory engineering requirement that every high-risk system project includes a logging module in its initial architecture specification, capturing the minimum event fields required by Article 12(3) before any code is written.\",\n\"how\": \"The compliance function issues a Logging Design Gate checkpoint; no project advances past design review until a logging schema covering start and end timestamps, reference databases, matched inputs, and verifier identities for Annex III point 1(a) systems is approved.\",\n\"why\": \"Building logs in at the architecture stage prevents costly retrofits and ensures the system technically allows automatic recording from day one, as Article 12 paragraph 1 demands.\"\n},\n{\n\"title\": \"Lifetime Log Retention and Access Infrastructure\",\n\"what\": \"A dedicated data infrastructure that stores system event logs for the full operational lifetime of each high-risk AI system, with access controls, integrity checks, and retrieval capability for regulatory inspections or post-market monitoring queries under Articles 72 and 26(5).\",\n\"how\": \"The lab provisions segregated storage environments per deployed system with cryptographic hashing of log entries to prevent tampering, retention schedules tied to the documented useful life of each product, and a query interface letting compliance staff pull event streams within hours.\",\n\"why\": \"Article 12 requires logs over the lifetime of the system; without purpose-built retention infrastructure, we cannot demonstrate traceability or respond to authority requests on any realistic timeline.\"\n},\n{\n\"title\": \"Post-Market Log Analytics and Alerting Function\",\n\"what\": \"A standing operational team that continuously analyses incoming event logs from deployed high-risk systems to detect risk-indicating patterns, flag situations that may trigger a substantial modification, and feed findings into the post-market monitoring plan required by Article 72.\",\n\"how\": \"We deploy automated anomaly-detection routines over the log streams, pair them with periodic human review cycles led by domain engineers, and produce monthly compliance dashboards showing which logged events map to Articles 79(1), 72, or 26(5) obligations for each deployed system.\",\n\"why\": \"Logs without analysis are inert; this function turns Article 12's recording mandate into an active surveillance capability that protects the lab from undetected drift and demonstrates proactive compliance to market-surveillance authorities.\"\n}\n]\n}",
