W systems are built (embedding logging infrastructure).\n- It also means adjusting timelines, adding compliance engineering roles, and potentially redesigning system architecture to accommodate logging.\n- I'd say this is a moderate-to-significant adjustment. Not 100 because it doesn't change the fundamental research direction. But it's more than 50 because if you get it wrong, you lose market access entirely. I'll go with 65 - it requires significant engineering changes and process rework but not a complete pivot in what the lab studies or builds.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The question is \"how much should the lab change its R&D agenda.\" If the lab's systems are high-risk, they MUST build logging capabilities. This could mean:\n- Redesigning system architecture to embed loggers\n- Adding data retention infrastructure (lifetime of system)\n- For Annex III 1(a): specific fields that must be captured\n- Post-market monitoring pipelines\n\nBut it doesn't mean stopping research on other topics or changing the fundamental AI approaches. It's an engineering and process layer added on top. I'll go with 60 - moderate to significant. You're adding substantial infrastructure requirements but not abandoning your core research direction.\n\nNow for 3 distinct proposals:\n\n1. Something about engineering/architecture: Build a \"logging-as-design\" framework into all system development pipelines. This is about the technical architecture change.\n\n2. Something about data governance/infrastructure: Establish lifetime data retention and audit infrastructure. The regulation says \"over the lifetime of the system\" - this means you need storage, access controls, retention policies that last potentially decades.\n\n3. Something about post-market monitoring operations: Create a dedicated operational capability for continuous monitoring and log analysis after deployment. This is about the ongoing operational function, not just building the logs but actually using them for Article