unt words carefully. Each proposal should be 60-110 words across all four fields combined.\n\nLet me write the reasoning first (3-5 sentences), then stance number, then proposals.\n\nReasoning (3-5 sentences):\nAs Lab Director, my primary concern is that compliance work doesn't cannibalize our research output or require us to hire a separate compliance engineering team. Article 12's \"automatic recording\" mandate can largely be met by extending the telemetry and experiment-tracking infrastructure we already operate for model monitoring, rather than building parallel logging systems from scratch. The real resource risk is scope creep: if every project team independently designs its own logging schema, we fragment our infrastructure and multiply integration costs. I therefore favor a thin middleware approach that standardizes log-emission at the inference layer while leaving domain-specific metadata to individual projects. This keeps the delta in engineering effort contained within roughly one quarter of existing MLOps headcount rather than triggering a multi-year re-platforming programme.\n\nThat's 5 sentences. Good.\n\nStance: 38/100 - moderate adjustment, leveraging existing infrastructure, not an overhaul but not business-as-usual either.\n\nNow the proposals:\n\nProposal 1: \"Retrofit Inference Telemetry for Article 12 Log-Emission\"\n- what: Extend our existing model-serving telemetry pipeline to emit structured, tamper-evident event logs (start/end timestamps, input hashes, reference database IDs) that satisfy Article 12(1) and the Annex III minimums without requiring per-project logging design.\n- how: Insert a lightweight middleware layer between our inference API gateway and the storage backend, configured per system class; validate output against a compliance schema in CI before any model promotion to production.\n- why: Reuses infrastructure we already pay for and keeps incremental engineering cost under one quarter of MLOps capacity.\n\nLet me count word