ve Logging for Biometric AI\",\n      \"what\": \"A research programme investigating cryptographic and differential-privacy techniques that let biometric identification systems satisfy Article 12(3) logging mandates without permanently retaining raw biometric inputs.\",\n      \"how\": \"We would develop k-anonymity and secure-aggregation schemes for the match-record fields in paragraph 3(c), prototype them on our current deployment, and publish results with formal privacy guarantees alongside compliance mappings.\",\n      \"why\": \"It addresses a genuine knowledge frontier—how to reconcile traceability obligations with data-minimisation principles—and gives the lab a distinctive publication profile.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Production Logs as Drift-Monitoring Research Corpus\",\n      \"what\": \"A methodology project that repurposes the mandatory Article 12 event streams into a longitudinal scientific dataset for studying performance degradation, concept drift, and edge-case accumulation in deployed high-risk models.\",\n      \"how\": \"We would build an internal analytics pipeline over our logging backends, define drift-detection benchmarks, and publish open benchmark suites so the broader community can validate post-market monitoring methods under Article 72 conditions.\",\n      \"why\": \"It converts a compliance artefact into a unique long-horizon research asset with direct impact on how the scientific community understands AI system ageing.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
