        "content": "{\n  \"reasoning\": \"From a development-engineering standpoint, Article 12 converts logging from an optional observability nicety into a hard architectural constraint that must be present at the first commit of every high-risk pipeline. The practical implication for prototyping speed is direct: we can no longer iterate freely on model logic and bolt on event emission in a pre-deployment sprint, because the schema, storage tiering, and retention semantics have to be settled before weights are frozen or else retrofitting becomes a multi-quarter migration. Technically, though, the mandated fields—timestamps, database pointers, input data, verifier identity—are straightforward to capture; the real engineering lift is in building a shared instrumentation layer that is cheap enough for early prototypes yet rigid enough to survive an audit years later. My main viability concern is not *whether* we can log but whether the logging overhead will silently tax every prototype cycle if we over-engineer it at day one, so the design goal is a thin SDK with strict schema contracts rather than a heavyweight APM stack. In short, this regulation changes how we structure and gate our build process; it does not alter which architectures or hypotheses we pursue.\",\n  \"stance\": 38,\n  \"proposals\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Compliance-Native Logging SDK\",\n      \"what\": \"A lightweight instrumentation library that emits structured, schema-versioned events into a local event bus from day one of prototyping, so compliance logging exists before model weights are frozen. Developers apply it via a single middleware decorator per service rather than scattering log calls through feature code.\",\n      \"how\": \"Two senior engineers build the core in six weeks against an internal JSON-schema registry for event types; the CI/CD pipeline gains a hard gate that blocks any prototype from reaching staging without emitting logs through the SDK, making compliance a compile-time concern rather than a deployment checklist item.\",\n      \"why\": \"It removes the retrofitting friction that historically slows our prototyping velocity and keeps logging invisible to feature developers, so iteration speed is preserved while every artifact is audit-ready by construction.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Biometric Log Retention Architecture\",\n      \"what\": \"A tiered storage design covering Article 12(3)'s four mandatory fields—use timestamps, reference-database pointer, matched input data, and verifier identity—with immutable append-only logs, role-based access controls, and a retention policy spanning the full operational lifetime of each biometric system.\",\n      \"how\": \"A three-month engineering spike builds a working prototype on WORM object storage, drafts per-field schemas validated against the regulation's minimum set, and stress-tests retrieval latency under simulated market-surveillance audit queries before any Annex III (a) product ships to an EU customer.\",\n      \"why\": \"Without this architecture pre-built into the deployment topology, our biometric systems cannot lawfully operate in the EU, and retrofitting immutable, access-controlled storage into live pipelines after go-live is prohibitively costly and reputationally damaging.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Event Taxonomy for Novel AI Pipelines\",\n      \"what\": \"A living classification framework that translates Article 12(2)'s abstract categories—risk situations, substantial modifications, operational anomalies—into concrete, machine-detectable event types for each of our pipelines, including novel architectures where failure modes are not yet characterised in the literature.\",\n      \"how\": \"Each project team files a short event specification alongside its model card before integration testing; a cross-team working group merges these into a central registry and runs quarterly reviews of production logs to capture newly observed failure classes that must be added as recordable events.\",\n      \"why\": \"Without concrete per-system event definitions we cannot write the instrumentation code in the SDK, because engineers need an explicit answer to what 'risk-relevant' means for their specific architecture before they can emit a compliant log entry.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
