ent. I'd say around 55-60. It's more than moderate because the technical architecture changes are real and must be built-in from design phase, but it doesn't require us to abandon our research agenda entirely—we're adding compliance infrastructure to existing workflows. Let me go with 58.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The article says \"shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\" This is an architectural constraint. If our R&D pipeline doesn't currently account for this, we need to restructure how we build systems. But it's not a 100—we're still doing AI R&D, just with compliance-by-design. I'll go with 55.\n\n**3 Distinct proposals:**\n\nThey need to be genuinely different:\n- One could be about engineering/architecture (building the logging infrastructure)\n- One could be about process/governance (updating development lifecycle and documentation)\n- One could be about external engagement/market positioning (working with regulators, or creating a compliance product feature that becomes marketable)\n\nLet me draft each:\n\n**Proposal 1: Engineering/Architecture focus**\nTitle: \"Embedded Logging Architecture for High-Risk Models\"\nWhat: A project to design and integrate automatic event-logging modules into every high-risk AI system before code freeze, capturing start/end timestamps, reference database identifiers, matched input data, and verifier identities.\nHow: The systems engineering team builds a standardized logging middleware layer that hooks into each model's inference pipeline, with schemas mapped directly to Article 12(3)(a)–(d), and runs automated regression tests confirming log integrity across the system lifetime.\nWhy: Without this technical capability we cannot legally place high-risk systems on the EU market, making it existential for institutional survival.\n\nLet me count words... \"A project to design and integrate automatic event-logging modules into every high-risk AI system before c