        "content": "{\n  \"reasoning\": \"As Lab Director, my first reaction is that Article 12 does not fundamentally alter what we research—our model architectures and scientific questions remain valid—but it does impose non-negotiable infrastructure obligations on every high-risk system we ship. The critical tension for us is cost: we operate under fixed public funding envelopes, and building persistent logging pipelines, maintaining technical documentation across a system's full lifetime, and staffing post-market monitoring all draw from the same budget that pays for compute cycles and doctoral salaries. The encouraging fact is that much of this maps onto infrastructure we already run (containerised serving stacks, experiment trackers, versioned datasets), so the appropriate response is targeted retrofit rather than greenfield construction. I am particularly wary of fragmentation: if every project team independently builds its own compliance logging, we burn scarce engineering talent and scatter data in ways that actually undermine the traceability the regulation exists to guarantee.\",\n  \"stance\": 35,\n  \"proposals\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Retrofit unified event-logging into existing pipelines\",\n      \"what\": \"We would build a shared, model-agnostic logging layer that sits beneath our current training and inference infrastructure, capturing start-and-end timestamps, reference-database versions, matched input records, and verifier identities automatically. This replaces the ad-hoc print statements and spreadsheet notes each project currently maintains in isolation.\",\n      \"how\": \"A dedicated two-person engineering pod embeds within the platform team for one quarter to design the event schema, integrate hooks into our containerised serving stack, and deploy it across all active high-risk system projects before end of Q3. The pod reports to me directly so that no single PI can deprioritise the integration under deadline pressure.\",\n      \"why\": \"Centralising this in one layer avoids every project reinventing its own logging, protecting our scarce engineering hours for actual research rather than compliance plumbing.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Design experiments to emit compliance logs natively\",\n      \"what\": \"We would revise our standard experimental protocol so that every model-evaluation run automatically produces the traceability artifacts Article 12 requires—timestamps, reference-database identifiers, matched inputs, verifier identities—as structured side-outputs rather than after-the-fact documentation assembled by a researcher at the end of a grant period.\",\n      \"how\": \"The research-methodology working group drafts updated experiment templates within two months; all new internal and external grant proposals must map their milestones to these templates, making traceability a design constraint from day one instead of a retrospective reporting burden. Peer review for project approval explicitly checks that the template mapping is complete.\",\n      \"why\": \"Baking traceability into the experimental loop means our post-market monitoring data already exists when a regulator requests it, eliminating the expensive late-stage documentation sprints that currently consume roughly ten percent of each project's closing-year budget.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Create a shared compliance-infrastructure cost centre\",\n      \"what\": \"We would establish a small lab-wide internal service—three full-time-equivalent staff—who own the logging stack, the technical-documentation repository, and lifetime file maintenance for all high-risk projects, with costs charged back to each project proportionally by compute usage.\",\n      \"how\": \"The budget office reclassifies eight percent of current per-project overhead into this shared pool starting next fiscal year; in exchange, individual principal investigators stop spending their own grant lines on ad-hoc compliance consulting and end-of-project documentation work. The service level agreement specifies a two-week turnaround for any regulator document request.\",\n      \"why\": \"Pooling fixed compliance costs across projects spreads the regulatory burden evenly, keeps any single project's funding intact for scientific ambition, and lets us leverage existing server infrastructure rather than purchasing new storage per team.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
