0-40. It's not business as usual (the logging requirements are specific and ongoing), but it's not a major overhaul either. We don't need to change what we research, just how we build and document. Maybe 35.\n\nNow for 3 distinct proposals:\n\n1. Infrastructure-focused: Build a unified logging/compliance layer into the existing ML pipeline infrastructure. This is about retrofitting our current R&D infrastructure.\n\n2. Process/workflow-focused: Redesign the documentation and record-keeping workflow so that compliance artifacts are generated as byproducts of normal research activities rather than as separate overhead tasks.\n\n3. Resource allocation/funding strategy: Reallocate a specific percentage of project budgets to absorb compliance costs without reducing scientific output, or create a shared compliance infrastructure team that serves all projects (economies of scale).\n\nLet me make these genuinely different:\n- Proposal 1: Technical infrastructure build (the logging system itself)\n- Proposal 2: Research methodology change (designing experiments so logs are generated natively)\n- Proposal 3: Organizational/funding structure change (shared compliance unit, budget reallocation)\n\nNow let me write these carefully with word counts. Each proposal must be 60-110 words total across title + what + how + why.\n\nLet me draft and count:\n\nProposal 1:\nTitle: \"Retrofit unified event logging into existing pipelines\" \nWhat: We would build a shared, model-agnostic logging layer that sits beneath our current training and inference infrastructure, capturing start/end timestamps, reference database versions, input-output pairs, and verification events automatically. This replaces the ad-hoc print statements each project maintains.\nHow: A dedicated two-person engineering pod embeds within the platform team for one quarter to design the schema, integrate hooks into our containerized serving stack, and deploy it across all active high-risk system projects by Q3.\nWhy: Cent