aceability(2) into(3) the(4) experimental(5) loop(6) means(7) our(8) post-market(9) monitoring(10) data(11) already(12) exists(13) when(14) regulators(15) ask(16), eliminating(17) expensive(18) late-stage(19) documentation(20) sprints(21) that(22) currently(23) eat(24) into(25) project(26) budgets(27). = 27 words\n\nTotal: 7 + 34 + 35 + 27 = 103 words. Good.\n\nProposal 3:\nTitle: \"Create a shared compliance-infrastructure cost center\" \nWhat: We would establish a small, lab-wide internal service—three FTEs—that owns the logging stack, documentation repository, and technical-file maintenance for all high-risk projects, charged back to each project proportionally by compute usage.\nHow: The budget office reclassifies 8% of current per-project overhead into this shared pool starting next fiscal year; in exchange, individual PIs stop spending their own grant lines on ad-hoc compliance consulting and documentation work.\nWhy: Pooling fixed compliance costs across projects spreads the regulatory burden evenly, keeps any single project's funding intact for scientific ambition, and lets us leverage existing server infrastructure rather than buying new storage per team.\n\nCount:\nTitle: Create(1) a(2) shared(3) compliance-infrastructure(4) cost(5) center(6) = 6 words\nWhat: We(1) would(2) establish(3) a(4) small(5), lab-wide(6) internal(7) service—three(8) FTEs—that(9) owns(10) the(11) logging(12) stack(13), documentation(14) repository(15), and(16) technical-file(17) maintenance(18) for(19) all(20) high-risk(21) projects(22), charged(23) back(24) to(25) each(26) project(27) proportionally(28) by(29) compute(30) usage(31). = 31 words\nHow: The(1) budget(2) office(3) reclassifies(4) 8%(5) of(6) current(7) per-project(8) overhead(9) into(10) this(11) shared(12) pool(13) starting(14) next(15) fiscal(16) year(17); in(18) exchange(19), individual(20) PIs(21) stop(22) spending(23) their(24) own(25) grant(26) lines(27) on(28) ad-hoc(29) compliance(30) consulting(31) and(32) documentation(33) work(34). = 34 words\nWhy: Pooling(1) fixed(2) compliance(3) costs(4) across(5) projects(6) spreads(7) the(8) regulatory(9) burden(10) evenly(11), keeps(12) any(13) single(14) project's(15) funding(16) intact(17) for(18) scientific(19) ambition(20), and(21) lets(22) us(23) leverage(24) existing(25) server(26) infrastructure(27) rather(28) than(29) buying(30) new(31) storage(32) per(33) team(34). = 34 words\n\nTotal: 6 + 31 + 34 + 34 = 105 words. Good.\n\nNow let me write the reasoning paragraph (3-5 sentences):\n\nAs Lab Director, my first reaction is that Article 12 doesn't fundamentally alter what we research—our model architectures and scientific questions remain valid—but it does impose non-negotiable infrastructure obligations on every high-risk system we ship. The critical tension for us is cost: we operate under fixed public funding envelopes, and building persistent logging pipelines, maintaining technical documentation across a system's full lifetime, and staffing post-market monitoring all draw from the same budget that pays for compute cycles and PhD salaries. The good news is that much of this maps onto infrastructure we already run (containerized serving stacks, experiment trackers, versioned datasets), so the response should be targeted retrofit rather than greenfield construction. I want to protect our scientific pipeline from fragmentation: if every project team independently builds its own compliance logging, we waste engineering talent and fragment data in ways that actually undermine the traceability the regulation seeks.\n\nThat's 5 sentences. Let me count: \n1. \"As Lab Director, my first reaction is...\"\n2. \"The critical tension for us is cost:...\"\n3. \"The good news is that much of this maps onto...\"\n4. \"I want to protect our scientific pipeline from fragmentation:...\"\n\nThat's only 4 sentences. Let me check again... Actually the colon in sentence 2 might be splitting it. Let me recount by periods/question marks/exclamation points at end of complete thoughts.\n\n1. \"As Lab Director, my first reaction is that Article 12 doesn't fundamentally alter what we research—our model architectures and scientific questions remain valid—but it does impose non-negotiable infrastructure obligations on every high-risk system we ship.\"\n2. \"The critical tension for us is cost: we operate under fixed public funding envelopes, and building persistent logging pipelines, maintaining technical documentation across a system's full lifetime, and staffing post-market monitoring all draw from the same budget that pays for compute cycles and PhD salaries.\"\n3. \"The good news is that much of this maps onto infrastructure we already run (containerized serving stacks, experiment trackers, versioned datasets), so the response should be targeted retrofit rather than greenfield construction.\"\n4. \"I want to protect our scientific pipeline from fragmentation: if every project team independently builds its own compliance logging, we waste engineering talent and fragment data in ways that actually undermine the traceability the regulation seeks.\"\n\nThat's 4 sentences. I need 3-5, so 4 is fine.\n\nNumber: I'll go with 35. It's a meaningful adjustment but not an overhaul. The R&D agenda (what we study) doesn't change; how we build and document changes significantly enough to require dedicated resources and process redesign.\n\nLet me finalize everything and make sure the formatting is clean, plain prose, no bullet lists inside fields.\n",
