tually, let me think more carefully. As Lab Director:\n- The logging requirement is relatively narrow in scope (automatic event logs with specific fields)\n- It doesn't change what we research or which models we pursue\n- But it does mean every high-risk system needs a logging middleware layer that persists over the \"lifetime\" of the system\n- This has implications for storage costs, infrastructure maintenance, and engineering time\n- The Annex III 1(a) requirements (biometric identification systems) are very specific about what must be logged\n- We need to think about whether our current deployment architecture can support this or if we need new infrastructure\n\nI'll go with a stance of around 45. It's not zero because there are real resource implications and we need to restructure some engineering teams' work, but it's not 100 because our core research agenda (what problems we solve, what architectures we explore) doesn't fundamentally change. The compliance burden is on the deployment/ops layer, not the research layer per se.\n\nNow for 3 distinct proposals:\n\n1. **Infrastructure-level**: Build a shared logging middleware/microservice that all high-risk systems can plug into. This is about effective use of existing R&D infrastructure—rather than each project team building its own logging, we create one central service. This saves resources across the lab.\n\n2. **Resource reallocation/prioritization**: Reallocate engineering FTEs from lower-priority research tracks to build and maintain compliance infrastructure. This is about the balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints—we need to explicitly decide what gets deprioritized. Or better: integrate logging requirements into our project selection criteria so that new projects budget for it from the start, reducing rework costs.\n\n3. **Leverage existing tools/processes**: Audit our current MLOps stack (we probably already use something like MLflow or internal tracking) and extend it to cover ope