gging obligations, creating dual-purpose research output with broad scientific impact on AI reliability literature.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Open Compliance Telemetry SDK and Log Benchmark\",\n      \"what\": \"An engineering project building an open-source software development kit that wraps model inference pipelines to capture the specific events mandated by Article 12(3), simultaneously producing a publicly released benchmark dataset of operational AI system logs.\",\n      \"how\": \"We would allocate a small dedicated team to design the SDK around our most-used high-risk prototype, instrument it with timestamped event recording for input data, reference databases, and verification actions, then release both code and an anonymized log corpus under permissive licensing.\",\n      \"why\": \"This converts a regulatory compliance cost into a shared scientific resource that the broader research community can use to study AI system failure modes and lifecycle behaviour at scale.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Scientific Documentation Standards for ML Systems\",\n      \"what\": \"A methodology research initiative interrogating Recital 71's call for clear documentation of algorithms, data, and validation processes, asking what constitutes scientifically rigorous description versus mere legal compliance in AI system technical files.\",\n      \"how\": \"We would convene internal workshops across our modelling and data-engineering teams to draft documentation templates and evaluation rubrics, then publish them as an open methodological paper with peer review from adjacent institutions.\",\n      \"why\": \"This elevates record-keeping from a bureaucratic afterthought into a genuine research contribution about reproducibility and epistemic transparency in machine science, strengthening the lab's intellectual standing.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
