debugging layer. So it's not a burden per se—it's a design requirement we should internalize into our prototyping toolkit.\n\nLet me settle on 35/100. It changes how we prototype (architecture decisions, middleware) but doesn't change what we research. The speed-of-prototyping angle means I want to make this as automated and template-driven as possible so it doesn't add friction to each new project.\n\n**Three distinct proposals:**\n\n1. **Reusable logging middleware / SDK** - Build a shared library/module that any high-risk system prototype can import, automatically capturing the mandated fields (timestamps, reference DB ID, input-match pairs, verifier IDs). This is an infrastructure/platform play. It's about reducing per-project engineering overhead and ensuring consistency.\n\n2. **Prototype-stage \"logging fitness\" test harness** - A CI/CD gate or test suite that validates whether a prototype correctly emits all Article 12 required log events before it can be promoted to integration testing. This is about quality gates in the prototyping pipeline. Speed of prototyping means catching logging gaps early, not at compliance review time.\n\n3. **Log schema versioning and storage cost modeling** - A technical study/pilot that models storage requirements over system lifetime for different use cases (e.g., biometric verification with millions of daily queries vs. a lower-volume clinical decision support tool), and establishes a log retention/compression strategy. This is about long-term viability and avoiding the \"storage architecture can't sustain it\" failure mode my Lab Director colleague flagged, but from the technical feasibility angle rather than budget allocation.\n\nLet me make sure these are genuinely different:\n- #1 is building shared infrastructure (platform engineering)\n- #2 is a quality/verification process in the dev pipeline (testing/QA)\n- #3 is a technical feasibility study and architecture decision (planning/modeling)\n\nYes, those are distinct. Let me write them up.\n\n**Stance score:** I'll go with 35/100. The regulation changes how we architect prototypes but not what we research. As a development engineer, I see the logging requirement as something that, if handled through shared tooling, actually improves our prototyping quality (better observability built in) rather than slowing us down.\n\nLet me now write the response in plain prose, keeping each proposal between 60-110 words total across its four fields.\n",
