tion into a genuine knowledge-frontier asset, letting us publish on real-world AI behaviour in ways lab-bench experiments cannot replicate.\"\n},\n{\n\"title\": \"Unified Validation Documentation as Publishable Science\",\n\"what\": \"A lab-wide methodology reform that elevates the technical documentation required by Recital 71—algorithms, data provenance, training and validation processes—into a standardized template that doubles as the methods section of our research publications and internal peer reviews.\",\n\"how\": \"The research-methods working group drafts a single scientific-record schema per project, reviewed at three gates (design, validation, post-deployment), so that compliance documentation and reproducible-science documentation are literally the same artifact maintained through the system's lifetime.\",\n\"why\": \"This removes the duplication cost our researchers currently feel between regulatory paperwork and publication requirements while raising the intellectual rigour of both outputs simultaneously.\"\n},\n{\n\"title\": \"Open Logging-Schema Benchmark for the Field\",\n\"what\": \"A broader-impact initiative in which we release an anonymized, versioned schema and synthetic dataset derived from our Article 12 logging architecture as a community benchmark for post-market monitoring methodology research.\",\n\"how\": \"After internal ethics review and GDPR-compliant de-identification, the compliance-engineering team packages the log structure (field definitions, temporal granularity, reference-database linkage) alongside a synthetic corpus, publishing it with an open licence on our institutional repository accompanied by a short methods paper.\",\n\"why\": \"It positions the lab as a scientific leader in operational-AI governance research rather than merely a compliant vendor, attracting collaborations and citations that compound our broader impact over time.\"\n}\n]\n}",
