mind, this could require significant rework. But for a well-run R&D institution, adding logging capabilities is more of an engineering task than a paradigm shift. I'd say around 40-55 range - moderate adjustment. Not a complete overhaul (not building different products), but a meaningful shift in how systems are architected and tested. Let me go with 48 or so. Actually, let me think... The requirement that logs span \"lifetime\" and the specific data fields for Annex III 1(a) could mean reworking existing prototypes. But it's not changing what we build, it's changing how we build it. I'll say 45 - a moderate adjustment, leaning toward the lower end because logging is an additive engineering requirement rather than a fundamental redesign of the AI capabilities themselves.\n\nNow for 3 distinct proposals:\n\n1. Something about embedding a unified logging architecture into all high-risk system development pipelines (engineering/architectural)\n2. Something about building a compliance documentation and traceability dashboard that satisfies Article 12 alongside technical documentation requirements (operational/process)\n3. Something about specific data governance for the Annex III 1(a) minimum fields - specifically handling the \"identification of natural persons involved in verification\" which has GDPR implications (data protection/legal)\n\nLet me make sure these are genuinely different:\n- #1 is about software architecture/engineering design pattern\n- #2 is about building an internal compliance tooling/platform\n- #3 is about a specific data governance protocol for sensitive biometric-related logging fields\n\nNow let me write each proposal with the four fields, keeping total between 60-110 words.\n\nLet me count carefully:\n\nProposal 1:\n- title: \"Embedded Lifetime-Logging Architecture in All Pipelines\" (8 words - good)\n- what: A mandatory design pattern requiring every high-risk AI prototype to integrate a standardized event-logging module before exiting the l