rection change.\n\nStance: I'd put this around 42-48. It's not zero because logging-as-infrastructure is a real engineering commitment that changes how we structure every project. But it's not 50+ because we're not changing what we research - we're changing the scaffolding we build on. The Lab Director and Researcher are at 45 and 42 respectively; I'm similar in magnitude but my reasoning is more about build-ability, prototyping velocity, and technical debt.\n\nLet me settle on 44. It's a moderate adjustment - we're not overhauling our research agenda, but we ARE making a structural engineering commitment that will affect every project's architecture from day one. The speed/quality tradeoff actually favors doing this now: building logging into the prototype skeleton is cheaper than retrofitting it after a system is in production.\n\nNow for 3 distinct proposals:\n\n1. A shared logging middleware/framework - This is infrastructure work. Build once, deploy everywhere. It's about prototyping speed because if every new project inherits logging by default, we save weeks of setup per project.\n\n2. A reference prototype for Annex III 1(a) biometric identification that demonstrates the four mandated fields in a working system - This is a concrete engineering deliverable that proves technical viability and gives us a template. It's different from #1 because it's domain-specific and validates the human-verification workflow integration.\n\n3. A logging schema validation CI/CD gate - This is about quality assurance in our development pipeline. Before any high-risk system goes to production (or even pre-production testing), automated checks verify that the required log fields are present, correctly formatted, and actually being written. Different from #1 (which builds the capability) and #2 (which validates a specific domain).\n\nLet me make sure these are genuinely distinct:\n- #1 is horizontal infrastructure (the plumbing all projects share)\n- #2 is vertical/domain-specific