        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to respond as a Lab Director at an R&D institution that develops AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. I need to:\n\n1. Reason in 3-5 sentences about how the lab should respond to Article 12 (Record-keeping).\n2. Give a number from 0-100 for stance on how much to change the R&D agenda.\n3. Propose exactly 3 distinct, concrete things the lab could do.\n\nLet me think carefully about my role: I'm a Lab Director concerned with resource-allocation efficiency, balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure. This is practical, budget-conscious thinking.\n\nMy perspective on Article 12:\n- The regulation requires automatic logging capabilities over the system lifetime\n- It specifies what events must be recorded (risk situations, post-market monitoring, operation monitoring)\n- For Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification), there are minimum requirements including start/end times, reference databases, input data for matches, and identification of verifying persons\n\nAs a Lab Director focused on resource efficiency:\n- This isn't a \"build new AI\" regulation—it's an infrastructure/engineering requirement. The lab already builds AI systems; now those systems need logging capabilities built in.\n- It affects how we design systems from the start (design-for-compliance) rather than retrofitting.\n- It creates ongoing operational costs (storing logs over lifetime).\n- It doesn't fundamentally change what science we pursue, but it changes how we engineer and deploy.\n- The resource-allocation concern: logging infrastructure, storage, data governance for the logs themselves (they contain personal data per 12(3)(d)).\n\nStance number: This is a compliance requirement on engineering practice, not a fundamental shift in what AI research to pursue. It's moderate—it changes how we build and deploy but doesn't redirect scientific ambition. I'd say around 30-40. N