erence-database pointers, matched inputs, and verifier identities in one schema. (~28 words)\nhow: Two platform engineers prototype it over one quarter within our existing CI/CD pipeline; project leads mandate integration at sprint zero for any Annex III system. (~25 words)\nwhy: One shared component amortises fixed cost across all projects, keeping per-project compliance overhead low enough that grant panels won't flag unallocated risk. (~23 words)\n\nTotal: 9 + 28 + 25 + 23 = 85. Good, within 60-110.\n\nProposal 2:\ntitle: \"Log Retention and Storage Cost Protocol\" \nwhat: A lab-wide operational protocol governing where lifetime logs are stored, what encryption applies, access tiers for the verifying-person data in Article 12(3)(d), and a five-year cost model tied to our existing cloud contracts. (~30 words)\nhow: The IT infrastructure team drafts it with external DPO consultation; finance validates the cost curve before we sign off on storage vendors next procurement cycle. (~25 words)\nwhy: Without this, lifetime log retention silently becomes an unbounded line item that erodes our R&D budget by year three, which is exactly the resource-drift I need to prevent. (~30 words)\n\nTotal: 7 + 30 + 25 + 30 = 92. Good.\n\nProposal 3:\ntitle: \"Traceability as a Day-One Design Constraint\" \nwhat: We restructure how new high-risk AI projects are scoped so that loggability and event-recording are treated as first-class technical requirements alongside accuracy, not retrofitted compliance add-ons discovered in review. (~28 words)\nhow: The project-intake template gains a mandatory traceability specification field; seed-funding decisions now require the team to demonstrate how their architecture will emit Article 12(2) events before any compute budget is released. (~30 words)\nwhy: Baking compliance into the research design from the start avoids costly re-architecture later and lets us bid on public contracts that already assume this capability, improving our funding win-r