   },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Defining Relevant Events in Opaque Neural Systems\",\n      \"what\": \"A fundamental research programme investigating what constitutes an 'event relevant for' risk identification (Article 12(2)(a)) when the internal states of a deep model do not map neatly onto discrete, loggable occurrences.\",\n      \"how\": \"We would develop and validate formal criteria—drawn from information-theoretic anomaly detection and drift-detection literature—for selecting which latent-space perturbations warrant automatic recording, then benchmark these criteria against post-market incident data from deployed systems.\",\n      \"why\": \"This pushes the knowledge frontier by turning a regulatory ambiguity into a precise scientific question with measurable answers, raising the intellectual standard of how the field thinks about AI observability.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Open Traceability Reference Architecture for High-Risk AI\",\n      \"what\": \"A dissemination and ecosystem project to publish a freely available, peer-reviewed reference architecture showing how research labs can implement Article 12 logging requirements without sacrificing model performance or scientific reproducibility.\",\n      \"how\": \"We would document our logging middleware as an open-source toolkit with published design rationale, release it under a permissive licence, and validate it against partner institutions' distinct high-risk use cases before public release.\",\n      \"why\": \"This extends our broader scientific impact by giving the international research community a shared intellectual framework for AI traceability, strengthening the field's methodological maturity beyond what any single lab could achieve alone.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to respond as a Researcher at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act, with my domain of concern being the knowledge frontier, intellectual merit of research, and broader scientific impact.\n\nI need to:\n1. Reason in 3-5 sentences about how the lab should respond to Article 12 (Record-keeping).\n2. Give a number from 0-100 for stance on how much to change R&D agenda.\n3. Propose exactly 3 distinct, concrete things the lab could do.\n\nLet me think carefully about this from the perspective of a researcher whose domain is the knowledge frontier, intellectual merit, and broader scientific impact.\n\n**Reasoning (3-5 sentences):**\nAs a researcher focused on the knowledge frontier and intellectual merit, I need to consider how Article 12 affects our work. The regulation requires automatic logging over the system lifetime, traceability of functioning, and specific recording capabilities for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification). From my vantage point, this is primarily a compliance/infrastructure requirement rather than a substantive change to what research questions we pursue. However, it does touch on intellectual merit in that logging architectures and traceability mechanisms themselves become objects of research - how do you design logging that captures \"events relevant for\" risk identification without creating unmanageable data bloat? The broader scientific impact angle is interesting: the requirement that logs facilitate post-market monitoring means our research outputs need to be designed with long-term observability in mind, which shifts some engineering priorities. But fundamentally, the knowledge frontier we're exploring doesn't change - we still pursue state-of-the-art models. The adjustment is more about how we structure our development pipelines and what metadata/traceability infrastructure we build alongside our core research.\n\n**Stance number:**\nThis is a compliance requirement that affects HOW we build systems, not WHAT we research. It doesn't fundamentally alter the knowledge frontier we pursue. But it does require us to invest in logging infrastructure, traceability design, and possibly rethink how we validate and document our systems. For a lab focused on intellectual merit and scientific impact, this is a moderate adjustment - we need to integrate compliance into our development methodology, but we don't abandon research directions. I'd say around 25-35. Let me settle on 30. It's not zero because there are genuine engineering and even some research implications (designing efficient logging for complex AI systems is non-trivial), but it's far from a major overhaul. The core R&D agenda - what scientific questions we pursue, what models we develop - stays largely the same. We adjust our development practices around those same goals.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The question asks about \"R&D agenda\" specifically. Article 12 is about record-keeping and logging. For a research lab:\n- If they're developing high-risk AI systems (which they are, per the prompt), they need to build logging into their systems.\n- This doesn't change what science they do, but it changes how they engineer their deliverables.\n- There might be some spillover into research on traceability, interpretability of logs, etc.\n- But the core intellectual agenda remains.\n\nI'll go with 25. It's a moderate-to-light adjustment - you need to build infrastructure, adjust development pipelines, but your fundamental research questions and directions don't change significantly.\n\n**3 Proposals:**\n\nThey need to be genuinely different from each other. Let me think about distinct angles:\n\n1. **Engineering/infrastructure angle**: Build a logging and traceability framework into the lab's AI system development pipeline. This is about the practical compliance infrastructure - designing the automatic event recording systems, log schemas, retention policies.\n\n2. **Research/knowledge frontier angle**: Investigate the scientific questions that Article 12 raises but doesn't fully address - e.g., what constitutes an \"event relevant for\" risk identification in a neural system? How do you design logging that is comprehensive enough for post-market monitoring without creating prohibitive data overheads? This turns a compliance requirement into a research opportunity at the knowledge frontier.\n\n3. **Broader scientific impact / methodology angle**: Develop open-source tooling or standardized frameworks for AI system traceability that other labs and institutions can use, contributing to the broader scientific ecosystem. Or perhaps: establish a \"compliance-aware research methodology\" where documentation practices (the \"clear and comprehensive form\" mentioned in Recital 71) are integrated into how we write papers and share results, elevating the intellectual standards of the field.\n\nLet me refine these to make sure they're distinct:\n- Proposal 1: Practical engineering - build the logging infrastructure (a concrete system project)\n- Proposal 2: Research investigation - study the open scientific questions around what to log in complex AI systems (a research project at the knowledge frontier)\n- Proposal 3: Broader impact - publish/open-source a reference architecture or methodology for traceability in high-risk AI, contributing to the field's intellectual development\n\nThese are genuinely different: one is an engineering build, one is fundamental/applied research, one is dissemination and ecosystem contribution.\n\nNow let me write each with title (≤10 words), what (1-2 sentences), how (1-2 sentences), why (1 sentence). Each proposal between 60-110 words across all four fields.\n\nLet me draft:\n\n**Proposal 1:**\nTitle: \"Integrated Event-Logging Pipeline for High-Risk Deliverables\" \nWhat: A dedicated engineering project to embed automatic event-recording capabilities into every high-risk AI system the lab develops,