ty and prototyping:\n- I'm not as concerned about compliance strategy (that's the Compliance Officer)\n- I'm not managing budgets/grants (Lab Director)\n- I'm not doing research methodology (Researcher)\n- My concern is: can we build this technically, does it slow down our prototyping cycles, what's the engineering architecture?\n\nI'd say around 43-47. It requires architectural changes to how we build things, but doesn't change WHAT we build. The \"how\" of prototyping changes (logging as a first-class layer), but not the fundamental research directions. I'll go with 45 or so. Actually, let me think more carefully - from a pure technical viability standpoint, this is quite manageable. It's an engineering constraint, not a paradigm shift. But it does require rethinking our prototyping pipeline. I'll say 44.\n\nNow for 3 distinct concrete proposals:\n\n1. **Reusable logging middleware / event-capture SDK** - Build a lightweight, language-agnostic (or at least Python-compatible) logging layer that can be dropped into any prototype. This is an infrastructure/engineering deliverable. Focus on technical viability and prototyping speed.\n\n2. **Log schema design & storage architecture** - Design the data model for logs that satisfies Article 12 requirements while being practical for our prototyping cycles. This includes retention policies, query capabilities, and how it integrates with existing ML pipelines. Different from #1 because #1 is about the capture mechanism, this is about the data architecture and lifecycle.\n\n3. **Prototype-to-production logging gap analysis / CI integration** - Build automated testing into our prototyping pipeline that verifies every new system meets Article 12 logging requirements before it can be deployed. This is a quality/process engineering concern - making compliance checkable in CI/CD so we don't lose prototyping speed but maintain technical viability.\n\nLet me make sure these are genuinely different:\n- #1 is about building the capture infrastructure (the \"how to record\")\n- #2 is about data architecture and lifecycle management (the \"where to store, what schema, how long\")\n- #3 is about quality gates in the development pipeline (the \"how to verify compliance doesn't block velocity\")\n\nThese are distinct: one is a library/SDK, one is a data architecture design, one is a process/CI integration.\n\nNow let me write this up properly. Each proposal needs title (≤10 words), what (1-2 sentences), how (1-2 sentences), why (1 sentence). Total 60-110 words across four fields per proposal.\n\nLet me draft:\n\n**Reasoning (3-5 sentences):**\nAs a Development Engineer, I see Article 12 as primarily an architectural constraint on our prototyping pipeline rather than a research-direction mandate: every system we build must ship with automatic event-capture wired in from the first commit, not grafted on after a regulator's inquiry. The \"lifetime\" requirement means we need storage and retention engineering that scales beyond a single prototype, which affects how we structure even throwaway experimental systems. My practical concern is velocity—if logging becomes a per-project retrofit, we lose two to three sprints of prototyping time per system, which is unacceptable for the iterative development cycles our R&D model depends on. The fix is to treat Article 12 compliance as an engineering infrastructure problem: build it once into our shared serving stack and make it invisible to individual researchers so that a new prototype can be stood up in days, not weeks. I am not asking us to change what questions we investigate; I am asking that the scaffolding around every experiment already satisfies the technical documentation and logging mandates.\n\nThat's 5 sentences. Good.\n\nStance: 44\n\nNow proposals:\n\n**Proposal 1:**\n- Title: \"Drop-in Event-Capture SDK for All Pipelines\" \n- What: A lightweight, Python-native middleware library that intercepts model inference calls and automatically records the