a Research Programme\"\n- title: Temporal Drift Detection as a First-Class Research Line (9 words) ✓\n- what: A multi-year research programme studying how to detect statistically meaningful shifts in AI system behaviour from operational logs over extended time horizons, treating the Article 12(2)(a)-(c) event categories as a structured signal space rather than mere compliance data. \n- how: We would recruit two postdoctoral researchers to formalise change-detection and anomaly-scoring methods on the structured logs our systems already produce, benchmarking against synthetic drift scenarios before applying to production telemetry.\n- why: This converts a regulatory logging obligation into a publishable contribution at the intersection of statistical process control and ML operations, advancing the knowledge frontier while satisfying Article 12(2)(b). \nTotal: 40 + 38 + 39 = 117 words. A bit over. Let me trim.\n\nLet me recount more carefully. I need to be between 60 and 110 words TOTAL across the four fields for each proposal.\n\nProposal 1:\n- title: \"Temporal Drift Detection as a Research Line\" (8 words) - wait, does the title count toward the word count? Let me re-read: \"Keep each proposal between 60 and 110 words across its four fields.\" The four fields are title, what, how, why. So yes, all four fields combined should be 60-110 words.\n\nLet me recount:\ntitle (8) + what (?) + how (?) + why (?) = 60-110 total.\n\nThat's tight. Let me aim for about 90 total per proposal.\n\nProposal 1:\ntitle: \"Temporal Drift Detection as a Research Line\" \nwhat: \"A multi-year programme studying statistically meaningful shifts in AI system behaviour drawn from operational logs, treating Article 12(2) event categories as a structured signal space rather than mere compliance data.\" \nhow: \"Two postdocs would formalise change-detection methods on the structured logs our systems already generate, benchmarking against synthetic drift scenarios before applying to production telemetry.\" \nwhy: \"This converts a logging obligation into a publishable contribution at the intersection of statistical process control and ML operations, advancing our knowledge frontier while satisfying traceability requirements.\" \nTotal: 8 + 29 + 25 + 30 = 92 words. Good, within range.\n\nProposal 2:\ntitle: \"Open-Source AI Provenance Schema for the Community\" \nwhat: \"A freely licensed metadata schema and reference implementation that extends Article 12 minimums into a general-purpose provenance standard for ML experiments, designed so any lab can adopt it without proprietary lock-in.\" \nhow: \"A small cross-disciplinary working group within our institution would draft the schema over two sprints, seed it with our own training and validation records as test data, and release under an Apache 2.0 licence on a public repository.\" \nwhy: \"This positions our lab as the origin point of a community standard, amplifying broader scientific impact far beyond any single compliance filing.\" \nTotal: 8 + 31 + 35 + 23 = 97 words. Good.\n\nProposal 3:\ntitle: \"Log-Native Experimental Design Methodology\" \nwhat: \"A restructured internal research protocol in which every experiment is designed so that audit-grade event logs are generated as a by-product of normal scientific workflow, eliminating the current gap between publication and compliance documentation.\" \nhow: \"We would pilot this across three active projects over one quarter, embedding structured logging hooks into our existing MLOps pipelines during design reviews rather than retrofitting after results are in.\" \nwhy: \"This raises the intellectual rigour of every paper we produce by ensuring full methodological provenance is available to reviewers and future researchers without extra authoring effort.\" \nTotal: 6 + 35 + 29 + 28 = 98 words. Good.\n\nLet me double-check these are genuinely different:\n1. A research programme (new scientific questions about drift detection) - knowledge frontier focus\n2.