odology, not a fundamental change in what we work on. I'll go with 45.\n\nNow for 3 distinct proposals:\n\n1. A reusable event-logging SDK/middleware that prototypes integrate early - this is an infrastructure/tooling project\n2. A compliance test harness / validation suite that automatically checks whether a prototype's logging meets Article 12 requirements before it leaves the lab - this is a QA/validation process change\n3. A \"lifetime storage\" architecture study or PoC for persistent, queryable log retention across system updates - this addresses the technical viability of the lifetime requirement\n\nLet me make sure these are genuinely different:\n- Proposal 1: Build the logging tool (infrastructure)\n- Proposal 2: Verify compliance automatically in CI/prototype pipeline (process/tooling)\n- Proposal 3: Solve the persistence/architecture challenge for long-term log storage (research/engineering)\n\nYes, these are distinct: one is about building a component, one is about verification/QA process, one is about solving an architecture problem.\n\nLet me write each proposal in 60-110 words across all four fields combined. Let me count carefully.\n\nProposal 1:\n- title: \"Plug-and-play Logging SDK for High-Risk Prototypes\" \n- what: A shared Python/TypeScript library that wraps our model inference and data-ingestion pipelines with structured event emitters conforming to Article 12(2)–(3), so any new high-risk prototype gains compliant logging in under a day of integration.\n- how: We extract the minimal event schemas (timestamps, reference DB pointers, input-match records, verifier IDs) into an open internal package, add thin decorators around our existing inference stubs, and ship it through our standard prototype scaffold so engineers import one line at project start.\n- why: It converts what would be weeks of per-project logging rework into a minutes-long setup, protecting prototyping velocity while making every deliverable regulation-ready by construction.\n\nLet me count words for proposal 1:\ntitle: Plug-and-play Logging SDK for High-Risk Prototypes (8 words) - not counted in the 60-110? Let me re-read: \"Keep each proposal between 60 and 110 words across its four fields.\" So title + what + how + why = 60-110 words total.\n\nLet me recount:\ntitle (8) + what (~45) + how (~45) + why (~30) = ~128. Too many. Let me trim.\n\nLet me rewrite more concisely:\n\nProposal 1:\n- title: \"Shared Logging SDK for High-Risk Prototypes\" \n- what: A shared library wrapping our inference and data pipelines with structured event emitters matching Article 12(2)–(3), giving any new prototype compliant logging in a day of integration. (28 words)\n- how: We codify the minimal event schemas into an internal package, add decorators around existing inference stubs, and ship it via our standard scaffold so engineers import one line at project start. (34 words)\n- why: It turns weeks of per-project logging rework into minutes of setup, protecting prototyping speed while making every deliverable regulation-ready by construction. (22 words)\n\nTotal: 7+28+34+22 = 91 words. Good, within 60-110.\n\nProposal 2:\n- title: \"Automated Article 12 Compliance Gate in CI\" \n- what: A CI pipeline stage that ingests a prototype's emitted event stream and asserts against the Article 12 checklist (timestamps present, reference DB recorded, verifier IDs captured) before the build is tagged releasable. (30 words)\n- how: We write schema validators as pytest fixtures in our existing test runner, feed them synthetic inference sessions during nightly runs, and fail the pipeline on any missing or malformed field. (29 words)\n- why: It catches logging gaps while they are still cheap to fix in a prototype rather than discovering them at audit time months later. (24 words)\n\nTotal: 8+30+29+24 = 91 words. Good.\n\nProposal 3:\n- title: \"Lifetime Log Persistence Architecture PoC\" \n- what: A focused proof-of-concept demonstrating a queryable, append-only log store