rds total across four fields, in plain prose, no bullet lists inside fields.\n\nLet me count words carefully for each:\n\nProposal 1: \"Logging-Aware Architecture Design for Frontier Models\"\n- title: Logging-Aware Architecture Design for Frontier Models (7 words) ✓ under 10\n- what: A research programme investigating how to embed automatic event-recording mechanisms natively within novel model architectures without introducing latency or information bottlenecks that degrade performance. The goal is to treat traceability as an architectural constraint rather than a post-hoc instrumentation layer.\n- how: We would prototype three candidate logging interfaces (attention-map snapshots, intermediate activation checkpoints, and decision-boundary markers) across two frontier architectures currently in our pipeline, benchmarking inference cost against the granularity of events captured under Article 12(2).\n- why: It positions the lab at the intersection of systems engineering and model design, producing methods that are both scientifically novel and directly regulatory-relevant.\n\nLet me count words: \ntitle: \"Logging-Aware Architecture Design for Frontier Models\" = 7 words\nwhat: \"A research programme investigating how to embed automatic event-recording mechanisms natively within novel model architectures without introducing latency or information bottlenecks that degrade performance. The goal is to treat traceability as an architectural constraint rather than a post-hoc instrumentation layer.\" \nLet me count: A(1) research(2) programme(3) investigating(4) how(5) to(6) embed(7) automatic(8) event-recording(9) mechanisms(10) natively(11) within(12) novel(13) model(14) architectures(15) without(16) introducing(17) latency(18) or(19) information(20) bottlenecks(21) that(22) degrade(23) performance(24). The(25) goal(26) is(27) to(28) treat(29) traceability(30) as(31) an(32) architectural(33) constraint(34) rather(35) than(36) a(37) post-hoc(38) instrumentation(39) layer(40). = 40 words\n\nhow: \"We would prototype three candidate logging interfaces (attention-map snapshots, intermediate activation checkpoints, and decision-boundary markers) across two frontier architectures currently in our pipeline, benchmarking inference cost against the granularity of events captured under Article 12(2).\"\nLet me count: We(1) would(2) prototype(3) three(4) candidate(5) logging(6) interfaces(7) attention-map(8) snapshots(9) intermediate(10) activation(11) checkpoints(12) and(13) decision-boundary(14) markers(15) across(16) two(17) frontier(18) architectures(19) currently(20) in(21) our(22) pipeline(23) benchmarking(24) inference(25) cost(26) against(27) the(28) granularity(29) of(30) events(31) captured(32) under(33) Article(34) 12(2)(35). = about 35 words\n\nwhy: \"It positions the lab at the intersection of systems engineering and model design, producing methods that are both scientifically novel and directly regulatory-relevant.\"\nCount: It(1) positions(2) the(3) lab(4) at(5) the(6) intersection(7) of(8) systems(9) engineering(10) and(11) model(12) design(13) producing(14) methods(15) that(16) are(17) both(18) scientifically(19) novel(20) and(21) directly(22) regulatory-relevant(23). = 23 words\n\nTotal: 7 + 40 + 35 + 23 = 105 words. That's within 60-110. Good.\n\nProposal 2: \"Open-Source Traceability Toolkit for the Research Community\"\n- title: Open-Source Traceability Toolkit for the Research Community (8 words) ✓ under 10\n- what: A publicly released, modular software framework that implements Article 12 logging requirements as a drop-in layer for common training and inference stacks, enabling any lab to achieve compliance without reinventing the wheel.\n- how: The team would build the toolkit in Rust and Python with plugin hooks for PyTorch and JAX ecosystems, document it against each sub-clause of Article 12(2) and (3), and publish versioned releases under an open licence with a companion white paper explaining design decisions.\n-