\n{\n\"title\": \"Lifetime Documentation Lifecycle Protocol\",\n\"what\": \"A standing internal governance process that assigns named ownership and fixed update cadences to the technical documentation required by Recital 71—covering algorithms, data provenance, training pipelines, and risk-management records—so no record goes stale as systems evolve in production.\",\n\"how\": \"We will create a versioned documentation register per system with quarterly compliance reviews; any code or model change that invalidates existing records triggers mandatory re-documentation before the next release ships to a client or operator.\",\n\"why\": \"Regulators expect documentation to remain current throughout the entire lifetime of the AI system, and gaps here constitute a direct basis for enforcement action plus reputational damage in public-procurement evaluations.\"\n},\n{\n\"title\": \"Event-Relevance Taxonomy per Intended Purpose\",\n\"what\": \"A research workstream that maps, for each high-risk product line, which specific operational events constitute the 'relevant' records under Article 12(2), calibrating logging scope to proportionality with intended purpose rather than capturing everything indiscriminately.\",\n\"how\": \"Our compliance team and lead engineers will co-author an event taxonomy per product, validated against the triggers in Articles 79(1), 72, and 26(5), then reviewed annually as intended purposes or Commission guidance shift.\",\n\"why\": \"Over-logging creates data-protection exposure and storage liabilities while under-logging fails traceability obligations; a purpose-calibrated taxonomy protects both our legal posture and operational efficiency.\"\n}\n]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to act as a Compliance Officer of an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act, specifically Article 12 on Record-keeping. I need to:\n\n1. Reason in 3-5 sentences about how the lab should respond.\n2. Give a number from 0-100 for stance on changing the R&D agenda.\n3. Propose exactly 3 distinct, concrete things the lab could do.\n\nLet me think carefully about my role: Compliance Officer concerned with institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation. This is not an engineer's perspective or a researcher's perspective—it's about ensuring we don't get fined, shut down, or lose our reputation in the EU market.\n\n**Reasoning (3-5 sentences):**\nAs Compliance Officer, I need to think about what this means for the lab. Article 12 requires automatic logging capabilities built into high-risk AI systems. This isn't something you bolt on after the fact—it's a technical requirement that affects system architecture from day one. For Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification), there are very specific minimum logging requirements. If we're developing or deploying such systems, non-compliance means we simply cannot operate in the EU market—this is existential for us. The recital emphasizes \"lifetime\" tracking and that documentation must be kept up to date. This means our R&D process needs to embed compliance from the design phase, not treat it as a checkbox before launch. I should also consider that post-market monitoring (Article 72) and operator obligations (Article 26(5)) create ongoing operational burdens that affect how we structure teams and resources.\n\nLet me count: that's about 5 sentences. Let me refine.\n\n**Number:** This is a record-keeping requirement for high-risk AI systems. It doesn't fundamentally change WHAT we build, but it significantly changes HOW we build (architecture must support logging) and what we need to maintain operationally. If our lab develop