model architectures, so we can quantify the speed penalty before committing to a design.\nHow: I'll build micro-benchmarks into the SDK that fire in parallel with our existing accuracy/latency test suite; each prototype iteration reports its p95 inference time with-logging versus baseline, and any architecture whose overhead exceeds 2% of wall-clock time flags for engineering review before prototyping continues.\nWhy: As a development engineer I need to know the concrete performance tax so I can choose between in-process logging, async batch writes, or sampled recording without guessing—keeping our systems fast while compliant.\n\nWord count:\nTitle: Logging(1) Overhead(2) Latency(3) Benchmark(4) Suite(5) = 5 words ✓\nWhat: A(1) repeatable(2) performance(3) test(4) harness(5) that(6) measures(7) the(8) per-inference(9) latency(10) and(11) throughput(12) cost(13) of(14) the(15) mandatory(16) logging(17) layer(18) across(19) our(20) model(21) architectures,(22) so(23) we(24) can(25) quantify(26) the(27) speed(28) penalty(29) before(30) committing(31) to(32) a(33) design(34). = 34 words\nHow: I'll(1) build(2) micro-benchmarks(3) into(4) the(5) SDK(6) that(7) fire(8) in(9) parallel(10) with(11) our(12) existing(13) accuracy/latency(14) test(15) suite;(16) each(17) prototype(18) iteration(19) reports(20) its(21) p95(22) inference(23) time(24) with-logging(25) versus(26) baseline,(27) and(28) any(29) architecture(30) whose(31) overhead(32) exceeds(33) 2%(34) of(35) wall-clock(36) time(37) flags(38) for(39) engineering(40) review(41) before(42) prototyping(43) continues(44). = 44 words\nWhy: As(1) a(2) development(3) engineer(4) I(5) need(6) to(7) know(8) the(9) concrete(10) performance(11) tax(12) so(13) I(14) can(15) choose(16) between(17) in-process(18) logging,(19) async(20) batch(21) writes,(22) or(23) sampled(24) recording(25) without(26) guessing—keeping(27) our(28) systems(29) fast(30) while(31) compliant(32). = 32 words\n\nTotal: 5 + 34 + 44 + 32 = 115 words. That's over 110. Let me trim.\n\nLet me revise Proposal 3:\nTitle: \"Logging Overhead Latency Benchmark Suite\" (5 words)\nWhat: A repeatable test harness measuring per-inference latency and throughput cost of the mandatory logging layer, so we quantify the speed penalty before committing to an architecture. (26 words)\nHow: Micro-benchmarks run alongside our existing accuracy/latency suite in every prototype CI cycle; each iteration reports p95 inference time with-logging versus baseline, flagging any design whose overhead exceeds 2% for engineering review. (34 words)\nWhy: I need the concrete performance number to choose between synchronous logging, async batch writes, or sampled recording without guessing—keeping systems fast while compliant. (23 words)\n\nTotal: 5 + 26 + 34 + 23 = 88 words. ✓ Good.\n\nLet me recheck Proposal 1 more carefully:\nTitle: \"Shared Logging SDK for High-Risk Prototypes\" - let me recount: Shared(1) Logging(2) SDK(3) for(4) High-Risk(5) Prototypes(6) = 6 words ≤10 ✓\n\nWhat field: \"A reusable event-recording library that auto-captures the Annex III 1(a) minimums (session timestamps, reference-database pointers, matched input vectors, verifier IDs) on every inference call, designed to drop into our existing model-serving stack without modifying model code.\"\nLet me recount: A(1) reusable(2) event-recording(3) library(4) that(5) auto-captures(6) the(7) Annex(8) III(9) 1(a)(10) minimums(11) (session(12) timestamps,(13) reference-database(14) pointers,(15) matched(16) input(17) vectors,(18) verifier(19) IDs)(20) on(21) every(22) inference(23) call,(24) designed(25) to(26) drop(27) into(28) our(29) existing(30) model-serving(31) stack(32) without(33) modifying(34) model(35) code(36). = 36\n\nHow: \"I'll spec the SDK against our current inference framework in two sprints, build it with a pluggable storage backend (local file for prototyping, cloud object store for production), and require all new high-risk prototypes to link it at repository-creation time via a CI gate.\"\nI'll(1) spec(2) the(3) SDK(4) against(5) our(6) current(7) inference(8) framework(9) in(10) two(11) sprints,(12) build(13) it(14) with(15) a(16) pluggable(17) storage(18) backend(19) (local(20) file(21) for(22) prototyping,(23) cloud(24) object(25) store(26) for(27) production),(28) and(29) require(30) all(31) new(32) high-risk(33) prototypes(34) to(35) link(36) it(37) at(38) repository-creation(39) time(40) via(41) a(42) CI(43) gate(44). = 44\n\nWhy: \"One engineering investment eliminates per-project logging rework, directly protecting our prototype iteration speed while making every system compliant from day one.\"\nOne(1) engineering(2) investment(3) eliminates(4) per-project(5) logging(6) rework,(7) directly(8) protecting(9) our(10) prototype(11) iteration(12) speed(13) while(14) making(15) every(16) system(17) compliant(18) from(19) day(20) one(21). = 21\n\nTotal: 6 + 36 + 44 + 21 = 107 ✓\n\nNow let me write the reasoning paragraph (3-5 sentences):\n\n\"The automatic recording requirement in Article 12(1) means my prototyping checklist gains a mandatory architectural layer before any model code is written—every high-risk prototype must wire structured event capture at day one, not bolt it on post-deployment. The Annex III 1(a) minimums are concrete engineering specs (timestamps, reference-database pointers, matched inputs, verifier IDs) that I can absorb as a shared SDK rather than per-project custom code, so the upfront build cost is bounded and the marginal cost per new prototype stays near zero. My main technical-viability concern is whether 'automatic' recording introduces unacceptable latency in our inference loop; if it does, we need async or sampled logging strategies validated against performance SLAs before we lock in an architecture. The lifetime obligation also means my prototype-to-production handoff process needs a log-schema continuity check so traceability doesn't break at deployment. Net effect on my role: one new 