r monitoring dashboards and any notified body can access, partitioned per deployed system instance.\" = 29 words (I removed the S3 example to keep it cleaner)\n\nWait, let me re-read: \"e.g., S3-compatible with object lock\" - I'll keep something concrete. Let me redo:\nhow: \"We stand up a write-once append-only object store with cryptographic content addressing behind a thin query API, partitioned per deployed system instance so retrieval by time window or input reference is index-friendly.\" = 32 words\n\nwhy: \"It satisfies the over-the-lifetime retention clause without forcing prototype teams to design for decades-long storage during development sprints, keeping our iteration speed intact while guaranteeing compliance at deployment.\" = 28 words\n\nTotal: 8 + 37 + 32 + 28 = 105. Good.\n\nProposal 3: \"Automated Article 12 Compliance Gate in CI Pipeline\"\n- what: A continuous-integration check that, before any prototype is tagged release-ready, replays a synthetic workload against the candidate build and verifies all mandatory log fields are present, correctly timestamped, and referenceable per Annex III point 1(a) minima.\n- how: We add a test stage to our CI runners that fires N synthetic inference requests through a staging endpoint, then runs an assertion script confirming start/end timestamps exist, the reference database identifier is non-null, matched input data is captured, and verifier IDs are logged for each result.\n- why: It catches logging regressions in hours rather than at audit time, protecting both prototyping velocity and the quality bar we hold ourselves to before handoff.\n\nLet me count:\ntitle: 9 words (ok)\nwhat: \"A continuous-integration check that before any prototype is tagged release-ready replays a synthetic workload against the candidate build and verifies all mandatory log fields are present, correctly timestamped, and referenceable per Annex III point one-a minima.\" = let me recount...\n\n\"A continuous-integration check that, b