r fields combined. Let me count carefully.\n\nProposal 1:\n- title: \"Composable Article 12 Logging SDK for Model Wrappers\" (9 words - good, under 10)\n- what: A lightweight Python/TypeScript library that wraps any model inference call and automatically emits structured event logs covering start/end timestamps, reference databases used, input data hashes, and verifier identifiers. It plugs into the lab's existing experiment runners without requiring model-level code changes.\n- how: We build it as a thin middleware layer using OpenTelemetry spans augmented with AI-specific attributes (model version, dataset provenance, match results). Each prototype in the lab adopts it via a single decorator or context manager, and logs stream to our internal event store.\n- why: It makes compliance a byproduct of normal development rather than a separate task, preserving prototyping speed while ensuring every system we ship already emits Article 12–conformant logs.\n\nLet me count words for proposal 1:\nTitle: \"Composable Article 12 Logging SDK for Model Wrappers\" - that's the title, not counted in the 60-110? Actually re-reading: \"Keep each proposal between 60 and 110 words across its four fields.\" The four fields are title, what, how, why. So I need to count all of them together.\n\nLet me recount:\nTitle: Composable(1) Article(2) 12(3) Logging(4) SDK(5) for(6) Model(7) Wrappers(8) = 8 words\n\nWhat: A(1) lightweight(2) Python/TypeScript(3) library(4) that(5) wraps(6) any(7) model(8) inference(9) call(10) and(11) automatically(12) emits(13) structured(14) event(15) logs(16) covering(17) start/end(18) timestamps,(19) reference(20) databases(21) used,(22) input(23) data(24) hashes,(25) and(26) verifier(27) identifiers.(28) It(29) plugs(30) into(31) the(32) lab's(33) existing(34) experiment(35) runners(36) without(37) requiring(38) model-level(39) code(40) changes.(41) = 41 words\n\nHow: We(1) build(2) it(3) as(4) a(5) thin(6) middleware(7) layer(8) using(9) OpenTelemetry(10) 