ning(32). = 32 words\n\nHow: The(1) lab(2) will(3) assign(4) a(5) named(6) documentation(7) owner(8) to(9) every(10) active(11) system(12), with(13) quarterly(14) review(15) checkpoints(16) tied(17) to(18) model(19) retraining(20) cycles(21), version(22) updates(23), or(24) any(25) substantial(26) modification(27), and(28) store(29) all(30) revisions(31) in(32) a(33) tamper-evident(34) document(35) management(36) system(37) accessible(38) to(39) notified(40) bodies(41) on(42) request(43). = 43 words\n\nWhy: Recital(1) 71(2) makes(3) clear(4) that(5) up-to-date(6) technical(7) documentation(8) is(9) the(10) backbone(11) of(12) traceability(13) and(14) compliance(15) verification(16); gaps(17) here(18) are(19) the(20) most(21) common(22) finding(23) in(24) notified-body(25) audits(26) and(27) would(28) halt(29) our(30) CE(31) marking(32). = 32 words\n\nTotal: 32 + 43 + 32 = 107 words. Good, within 60-110.\n\nProposal 3:\n- Title: \"Log-Triggered Post-Market Monitoring Dashboard\" (5 words) ✓\n- What: A continuous monitoring toolset that ingests the automatic event logs produced by deployed high-risk systems and flags patterns indicating a risk under Article 79(1) or a drift toward substantial modification, feeding directly into our post-market monitoring obligations. \n- How: The lab will build an internal analytics service that consumes log streams from all in-field deployments, applies configurable alerting rules mapped to the intended purpose of each system, and generates monthly compliance reports for the technical documentation file and, where thresholds are breached, immediate notifications to deployers.\n- Why: Article 12(2) explicitly ties logging to post-market monitoring; having a live operational view of our deployed systems turns a static regulatory obligation into an active safety net that protects users and shields the institution from liability.\n\nLet me count:\nWhat: A(1) continuous(2) monitoring(3) toolset(4) that(5) ingests(6) the(7) automatic(8) ev