        "content": "{\n\"reasoning\": \"As a research institution whose core mission is advancing the knowledge frontier, I read Article 12 not as a constraint on what we may study but as a specification that reframes transparency from an afterthought into a first-class design property of every system we build. The requirement that high-risk AI technically allow for automatic recording over its full lifetime means our architectural choices—how inference is structured, where state lives, how data traverses the network—must be made with traceability as a co-designer rather than a compliance patch applied post hoc. Intellectually this is stimulating: it opens genuine open questions about differentiable logging mechanisms, information-theoretic limits of audit trails, and whether the act of recording events measurably perturbs system behaviour in ways we have not characterised before. The broader scientific-impact argument is equally strong; if well-designed logs reveal novel insights into model dynamics that periodic re-testing cannot surface, we convert a regulatory duty into a sustained publication pipeline and a contribution to AI-reliability science. That said, the lab's R&D agenda need not be overhauled—our frontier questions remain intact—but the how-we-build layer shifts meaningfully toward embedded traceability and lifetime-aware evaluation from day one of each project.\",\n\"stance\": 45,\n\"proposals\": [\n{\n\"title\": \"Intrinsic Traceability as a Model Architecture Property\",\n\"what\": \"A research programme that embeds event-logging directly into network computation graphs so that every inference step produces a structured, interpretable record without external instrumentation or measurable performance penalty.\",\n\"how\": \"We would formalise logging as an auxiliary loss term during training and design differentiable audit nodes within transformer and diffusion architectures, benchmarking them against post-hoc probing methods on standard tasks to quantify the fidelity-versus-throughput trade-off.\",\n\"why\": \"This moves the lab to the frontier of transparent-by-design AI and produces publications that define how traceability should be architecturally implemented rather than retrofitted, establishing our group as the reference architecture.\"\n},\n{\n\"title\": \"Open Log-Fidelity Benchmark for High-Risk AI\",\n\"what\": \"A public benchmark and tooling kit that measures whether a system's automatic logs actually capture the event categories mandated by Article 12(2), quantifying the gap between regulatory intent and engineering practice.\",\n\"how\": \"We would construct synthetic deployment scenarios, instrument open-weight models with candidate logging stacks, and release evaluation metrics plus a CI-ready test harness under an open licence for reuse by other labs and national regulators.\",\n\"why\": \"It positions the lab as the reference point for traceability science and gives the broader community a shared yardstick, amplifying our scientific impact well beyond compliance check-boxes.\"\n},\n{\n\"title\": \"Lifetime-Drift Studies from Mandatory Operational Logs\",\n\"what\": \"A longitudinal research programme that treats the automatic logs required by Article 12(1) as a scientific instrument for studying how high-risk AI models drift, degrade, or behave unexpectedly across months and years of real deployment.\",\n\"how\": \"Partnering with deployers of biometric identification systems under Annex III point 1(a), we would collect anonymised log streams over multi-year horizons and develop statistical methods that detect subtle behavioural shifts invisible to periodic re-testing.\",\n\"why\": \"This converts a compliance obligation into a unique large-scale observational dataset that no other group can replicate, opening a new empirical frontier in AI reliability science.\"\n}\n]\n}",
