sting properties about how the system learns and makes decisions?\n\nFor my stance number: As a researcher focused on knowledge frontier and scientific impact, I'd say this regulation pushes toward more structured, transparent development. It's not asking us to stop doing research, but it does require that our systems be built with traceability in mind from the start (not retrofitted). For high-risk AI specifically, there are specific logging requirements. But for a lab developing various AI systems, many of which may or may not fall under \"high-risk,\" the impact varies. I'd say moderate adjustment - maybe 40-55 range. Let me think... The regulation is prescriptive about what must be logged but doesn't fundamentally change WHAT we research. It changes HOW we build and document. For a lab that's primarily doing frontier research, the compliance layer is additive rather than transformative. But if our outputs are going to be deployed as high-risk systems, then architecture choices matter from day one. I'll go with 45 - moderate adjustment, not a major overhaul but a meaningful shift in how we approach system design and documentation.\n\nNow for 3 distinct proposals:\n\n1. Something about designing \"intrinsic traceability\" into model architectures - making the log a first-class research object, not an afterthought. This is about novel ML architecture work where the logging mechanism itself reveals something scientifically interesting about decision-making processes.\n\n2. Something about building an open-source compliance-testing framework or benchmark for evaluating whether logging mechanisms actually capture the right information without degrading performance. This is about creating tools/methods that serve the broader scientific community.\n\n3. Something about studying the \"epistemic value of logs\" - using the mandatory logging as a research instrument to study how AI systems behave over their lifetime, drift detection, etc. This turns a compliance burden into a scientific opportunity for understanding model degradation and behavior.\n\nLet me make sure these are genuinely different:\n- #1 is about architecture design (building traceability INTO the model)\n- #2 is about tooling/benchmarks (creating evaluation infrastructure for others)\n- #3 is about using logs as research instruments (post-deployment science)\n\nNow let me write each proposal in 60-110 words total across title, what, how, why. Let me count carefully.\n\nProposal 1:\nTitle: \"Intrinsic Traceability as a Model Architecture Property\" \nWhat: A research programme that embeds event-logging directly into network computation graphs so that every inference step produces a structured, interpretable record without external instrumentation or performance penalty. (27 words)\nHow: We would formalise logging as an auxiliary loss term during training and design differentiable \"audit nodes\" within transformer and diffusion architectures, benchmarking them against post-hoc probing methods on standard tasks. (31 words)\nWhy: This moves the lab to the frontier of transparent-by-design AI and produces publications that define how traceability should be architecturally implemented rather than retrofitted. (24 words)\n\nTotal: 7+27+31+24 = 89 words. Good, within 60-110.\n\nProposal 2:\nTitle: \"Open Log-Fidelity Benchmark Suite for High-Risk AI\" \nWhat: A public benchmark and tooling kit that measures whether a given system's automatic logs actually capture the event categories mandated by Article 12(2)(a)–(c), quantifying gaps between regulatory intent and engineering reality. (34 words)\nHow: We would build a set of synthetic and real deployment scenarios, instrument open-weight models with candidate logging stacks, release evaluation metrics and a CI-ready test harness under an open licence so other labs and regulators can reuse it. (38 words)\nWhy: It positions the lab as the reference point for traceability science and gives the broader research community a shared yardstick, amplifying our scientific impact well beyond compliance check-boxes. (31 words)\n\nTotal: 8+34+38+31 = 111 words. One over. Let me trim.\n\nLet me revise:\nTitle: \"Open Log-Fidelity Benchmark for High-Risk AI\" \nWhat: A public benchmark and tooling kit that measures whether a system's automatic logs capture the event categories mandated by Article 12(2), quantifying gaps between regulatory intent and engineering practice. (30 words)\nHow: We would construct synthetic deployment scenarios, instrument open-weight models with candidate logging stacks, and release evaluation metrics plus a CI-ready test harness under an open licence for reuse by other labs and regulators. (35 words)\nWhy: It positions the lab as the reference point for traceability science and gives the broader community a shared yardstick, amplifying our scientific impact beyond compliance check-boxes. (27 words)\n\nTotal: 8+30+35+27 = 100 words. Good.\n\nProposal 3:\nTitle: \"Lifetime-Drift Studies from Mandatory Operational Logs\" \nWhat: A longitudinal research programme that treats the automatic logs required by Article 12(1) as a scientific instrument for studying how high-risk AI models drift, degrade, or behave unexpectedly across months and years of real deployment. (38 words)\nHow: Partnering with deployers of biometric identification systems (Annex III 1(a)), we would collect anonymised log streams over multi-year horizons and develop statistical methods that detect subtle behavioural shifts invisible to periodic re-testing. (32 words)\nWhy: This converts a compliance obligation into a unique, large-scale observational dataset that no other group can replicate, opening a new empirical frontier in AI reliability science. (27 words)\n\nTotal: 6+38+32+27 = 103 words. Good.\n\nNow let me write the 3-5 sentence reasoning and assign my number.\n\nLet me draft the full response now.\n\nReasoning (3-5 sentences):\nAs a research institution whose core mission is advancing the knowledge 