oards and market-surveillance responses pull data directly rather than reconstructing it from ad-hoc scripts.\nWhy: It turns the \"lifetime\" retention obligation in Recital 71 into a boring infrastructure component we build once, removing per-project storage planning from every prototype's critical path.\n\nCount: 8 + 35 + 46 + 28 = 117. Slightly over. Trim.\n\nProposal 3 revised:\nTitle: \"Durable Event-Stream Store for Lifetime Log Retention\" (8 words)\nWhat: A time-indexed, append-only storage layer on our existing object-storage cluster that retains structured event logs across each system's full operational lifetime and supports sub-minute replay queries by date, event type, or session. \nHow: We model it as partitioned immutable segments per deployed instance with a thin query API, so post-market monitoring dashboards and market-surveillance responses pull data directly rather than reconstructing it from ad-hoc scripts.\nWhy: It turns Recital 71's lifetime retention obligation into a build-once infrastructure component, removing per-project storage planning from every prototype's critical path.\n\nCount: 8 + 36 + 35 + 24 = 103. Good.\n\nLet me finalize and make sure everything reads well in plain prose, no bullets inside fields.\n",
