rs from compliance overhead while satisfying the architectural mandate the regulation demands.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Ring-Fenced Compliance Engineering Budget Line\",\n      \"what\": \"A dedicated annual funding allocation—roughly eight to twelve percent of total R&D spend—earmarked specifically for regulatory engineering tasks such as logging infrastructure, log-audit tooling, and operator-oversight dashboards, so these stop competing ad-hoc with grant-funded science.\",\n      \"how\": \"The lab council adopts this as a standing budget category next fiscal year; each project's work plan must explicitly log hours and compute allocated to Article 12 obligations against that line, giving the directorate clean visibility into where compliance costs land.\",\n      \"why\": \"Making the compliance cost visible, predictable, and defensible in grant-renewal narratives prevents silent erosion of research time and protects us from mid-cycle budget shocks when a new system enters scope.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Research Programme on Optimal AI Logging Architectures\",\n      \"what\": \"A small internal research track studying which event-capture granularity, retention horizons, and log-structure designs best serve the distinct high-risk categories under Annex III, producing open benchmarks and reusable design patterns for the community.\",\n      \"how\": \"We dedicate one postdoc position and modest GPU compute for eighteen months, targeting publication in AI-governance venues to attract EU Horizon or Digital Europe co-funding that offsets most of the core personnel cost.\",\n      \"why\": \"Turning a regulatory constraint into publishable science and a potential new revenue stream lets us honour the letter of Article 12 while preserving scientific ambition within tight fiscal ceilings.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
