        "content": "{\n  \"reasoning\": \"Article 12 is not a documentation formality; it imposes a hard architectural requirement that high-risk systems technically allow automatic event recording over their full lifetime, and Recital 71 reinforces that the associated technical file must stay current across that same period. For our lab this means every system currently in development or deployed for EU customers needs an auditable log stream covering the specific events listed in paragraphs 2(a)–(c), with additional mandatory fields—use timestamps, reference database, matched input data, verifier identity—if we touch Annex III point 1(a) biometric work. The immediate operational risk is that several of our deployed prototypes predate this obligation and would fail an Article 72 post-market monitoring review if a regulator requested the logs. I do not read this as requiring us to abandon any research line; the obligation shapes engineering practice, governance cadence, and how we present our systems to procurement teams rather than what models or algorithms we pursue. The reputational upside is real: in a market where EU public-sector buyers will soon demand proof of Article 12 compliance at tender stage, being early and visibly compliant is a competitive asset.\",\n  \"stance\": 42,\n  \"proposals\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Standardized Compliance Logging Layer for All High-Risk Systems\",\n      \"what\": \"A mandatory architectural module that auto-records events (timestamps, input data, database references, verification actor IDs) into an immutable log store for every high-risk system the lab ships. It must be present from the first sprint of any new project, not retrofitted after deployment.\",\n      \"how\": \"The engineering team designs a shared logging SDK with hooks at defined event points; CI/CD pipelines gate deployment on the presence and correctness of log emissions verified by integration tests in every build.\",\n      \"why\": \"It converts an Article 12 obligation into a single reusable component, reducing per-project compliance cost and eliminating the risk that any system ships without required recording capabilities.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Lifetime Technical Documentation Office with Named Owners\",\n      \"what\": \"A dedicated internal function that authors, versions, and updates the technical documentation required by Recital 71—algorithms, data provenance, testing records, risk-management descriptions—for every high-risk system across its operational lifetime. Each product line has a named owner with accountability.\",\n      \"how\": \"The office reports to my compliance function, embedding within project teams during development then transitioning to operations post-deployment. Mandatory quarterly reviews trigger on any substantial modification or new log event type.\",\n      \"why\": \"It ensures the lab can produce a current technical file for regulators on demand, protecting against enforcement and preserving our market reputation during audits.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Client-Facing Audit Log API as Market Differentiator\",\n      \"what\": \"We expose a secure read-only interface letting corporate clients query the Article 12 event logs of their deployed systems—timestamps, matched inputs, verification actor IDs—turning a regulatory obligation into a transparency feature competitors cannot easily replicate.\",\n      \"how\": \"The logging SDK from Proposal 1 emits structured events to a cloud store; we add a thin REST API and dashboard, scoped by contract so each customer sees only their system's records with standard-format export for their own audits.\",\n      \"why\": \"It converts a compliance cost centre into a revenue-adjacent capability, giving enterprise buyers a concrete reason to select our systems in procurement.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
