at compliant system design actually means across the field.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Operational Logs as a Scientific Instrument\",\n      \"what\": \"A methodology project reframing the mandatory continuous logging of high-risk systems as a longitudinal observational dataset, enabling new statistical methods for detecting capability drift, emergent failure modes, and distributional shift in deployed models over months or years.\",\n      \"how\": \"We instrument our own lab deployments to capture six-month rolling logs, then develop change-detection and anomaly-attribution algorithms validated against known injection faults, publishing both the benchmark dataset and the detection toolkit under an open licence.\",\n      \"why\": \"It turns a regulatory data stream into genuine scientific infrastructure that advances post-market monitoring as an empirical discipline rather than a bureaucratic checkbox.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Open Technical Documentation Standards for AI Research\",\n      \"what\": \"A standards effort producing open, machine-readable schemas for the technical documentation that Recital 71 requires—covering capabilities, limitations, training-data provenance, and risk-management decisions—so that compliance records double as citable scientific artifacts.\",\n      \"how\": \"We draft three schema proposals with input from our engineering team and two external labs, run a six-month pilot on real high-risk projects, then submit the schemas to a European standardisation body and open-source the surrounding tooling.\",\n      \"why\": \"It serves broader scientific impact by making the knowledge embedded in compliance records reusable, searchable, and comparable across institutions, strengthening the collective research record.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
