eds into post-market monitoring dashboards (operational/organizational new capability)\n\nLet me draft these and count words carefully.\n\nProposal 1:\nTitle: \"Embed Traceability Logging in Core System Architecture\" \nWhat: We will develop a standardized, reusable logging middleware layer that captures event data automatically across all high-risk AI system deployments, satisfying Article 12(1)-(3) requirements by design rather than as an afterthought. (30 words)\n\nHow: The platform engineering team will build an open-source-style internal SDK that hooks into our inference pipelines and model-serving infrastructure, writing timestamped, immutable event records to a centralized audit store before any system enters production. (35 words)\n\nWhy: This eliminates per-project compliance rework and ensures every future high-risk release ships with the mandatory logging capability already baked in, protecting us from market-access delays and enforcement exposure. (29 words)\n\nTotal: 10 + 30 + 35 + 29 = 104 words. Good, within 60-110.\n\nWait, let me recount more carefully.\n\nTitle: \"Embed Traceability Logging in Core System Architecture\" - that's the title field\nWhat: \"We will develop a standardized, reusable logging middleware layer that captures event data automatically across all high-risk AI system deployments, satisfying Article 12(1)-(3) requirements by design rather than as an afterthought.\" \nHow: \"The platform engineering team will build an internal SDK that hooks into our inference pipelines and model-serving infrastructure, writing timestamped, immutable event records to a centralized audit store before any system enters production.\" \nWhy: \"This eliminates per-project compliance rework and ensures every future high-risk release ships with the mandatory logging capability already baked in, protecting us from market-access delays and enforcement exposure.\" \nTotal: 10 + 32 + 33 + 28 = 103. Good.\n\nProposal 2:\nTitle: \"Redesign Biometric Verification Workflows for Annex III Compliance\" \nWait - is that more than 10? Let me count: Re-design(1) Biometric(2) Verification(3) Workflows(4) for(5) Annex(6) III(7) Compliance(8). That's 8 words. Good.\n\nWhat: For systems falling under Annex III point 1(a), we will restructure the human-in-the-loop verification process so that every use session is bracketed by start and end timestamps, reference-database identifiers are logged at query time, matched inputs are recorded, and verifier identities are captured per Article 14(5).\n\nHow: Product managers will work with our biometrics division to map each user journey against the four minimum data fields in Article 12(3), then update our verification UI and backend APIs so those fields are collected as a prerequisite of system output rather than as optional metadata.\n\nWhy: Non-compliance on these specific fields would render our biometric products legally unmarketable in the EU, so redesigning the workflow now preserves our market position and client contracts without waiting for an enforcement action to force the change.\n\nLet me count words:\nTitle: \"Redesign Biometric Verification Workflows for Annex III Compliance\" = 8 words\nWhat: \"For systems falling under Annex III point 1(a), we will restructure the human-in-the-loop verification process so that every use session is bracketed by start and end timestamps, reference-database identifiers are logged at query time, matched inputs are recorded, and verifier identities are captured per Article 14(5).\" = let me count: For(1) systems(2) falling(3) under(4) Annex(5) III(6) point(7) 1(a)(8), we(9) will(10) restructure(11) the(12) human-in-the-loop(13) verification(14) process(15) so(16) that(17) every(18) use(19) session(20) is(21) bracketed(22) by(23) start(24) and(25) end(26) timestamps(27), reference-database(28) identifiers(29) are(30) logged(31) at(32) query(33) time(34), matched(35) inputs(36) are(37) recorded(38), and(39) verifier(40) identities(41) are(42) capt