\n      \"what\": \"We establish a mandatory living-documentation workflow in which every high-risk project maintains up-to-date technical records covering algorithms, training data provenance, testing results, and risk-management outputs, as Article 12 and Recital 71 require. The document is version-controlled and updated at each experimental milestone.\",\n      \"how\": \"I will designate one research manager per project to own the documentation cycle, piggybacking on our existing Git-based lab notebook and internal wiki so no new software purchase is needed; a brief monthly review slot goes into team meetings.\",\n      \"why\": \"This keeps us audit-ready without creating a parallel bureaucracy that would divert scarce graduate-student hours from actual experimentation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Privacy-Aware Minimal-Log Research Workstream\",\n      \"what\": \"We open a small funded sub-project investigating logging architectures that capture the Article 12(2) event categories—risk indicators, post-market signals, operational anomalies—while minimising stored personal data and computational overhead. The goal is a publishable methodology we can licence back to industry partners.\",\n      \"how\": \"Two PhD candidates will prototype differential-privacy and hash-based log summarisation techniques on our existing biometric verification testbed, reporting progress at the annual lab review so I can assess whether the thread warrants further funding next cycle.\",\n      \"why\": \"This turns a compliance constraint into a genuine scientific contribution that strengthens our publication record and positions the lab as an authority regulators may consult.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
