        "content": "{\n  \"reasoning\": \"As Compliance Officer, I read Article 12 not as a paperwork obligation but as an engineering constraint that must be satisfied at the architectural level—logging is a system property we build in, not a feature we bolt on when a regulator asks. The \\\"lifetime\\\" qualifier in paragraph 1 and Recital 71 means our retention, storage, and accessibility commitments extend across every version iteration of each high-risk model, which carries real cost implications for our infrastructure budget that I must flag to finance before the next planning cycle. If any portfolio item falls under Annex III point 1(a), biometric identification, the specific minimum fields in paragraph 3 mean dedicated data-engineering effort is required just to capture reference-database provenance and verification-personnel identity alongside basic session timestamps. Non-compliance here is not a fine line; it is a market-access line, because Article 99 penalties calibrated at up to seven percent of global turnover are designed to make non-conformity economically fatal for institutions our size. I will be recommending to the board that we treat conformance with paragraphs 1 through 3 as a gating criterion: no high-risk system enters pilot or deployment until its logging layer passes an internal check against the full article.\",\n  \"stance\": 62,\n  \"proposals\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Embed Logging Architecture as a Design Constraint\",\n      \"what\": \"We integrate an automated event-recording layer into every high-risk system's reference architecture so that start/end timestamps, reference databases, and match data are captured natively rather than retrofitted after deployment.\",\n      \"how\": \"Our platform team drafts a mandatory logging interface specification that each R&D group must implement before code review passes, backed by a shared library of primitives with schema validation against the Annex III minimum fields.\",\n      \"why\