e in any regulator inquiry. (25 words)\n\nTotal: 6 + 38 + 33 + 25 = 102. Good.\n\nProposal 3:\nTitle: \"Biannual Logging-Conformity Self-Audit Protocol\" (5 words)\nWhat: We create a recurring internal audit cycle in which an independent compliance team verifies that our logging systems actually capture the event categories required by Article 12(2)(a)-(c)—risk-identifying situations, post-market monitoring signals, and operational traces for Article 26(5). The audit tests whether logs would withstand scrutiny from a market-surveillance authority.\nHow: We write a standardised test script that injects synthetic events into staging environments and checks whether the required fields appear in the log stream; findings feed a corrective-action register with named owners and deadlines reported to the board quarterly.\nWhy: Self-identifying gaps before a notified body or national authority does so protects our market reputation and avoids the compounding cost of a formal non-conformity proceeding.\n\nCount: Title (5) + What (~48) + How (~39) + Why (~27) = ~119. Too long. Trim.\n\nWhat: We create a recurring internal audit in which an independent compliance team verifies our logging systems capture the event categories required by Article 12(2)(a)-(c)—risk situations, post-market monitoring signals, and operational traces—testing whether logs would withstand market-surveillance scrutiny. (39 words)\nHow: A standardised test script injects synthetic events into staging environments to confirm required fields appear in the log stream; findings feed a corrective-action register with named owners and deadlines reported to the board quarterly. (35 words)\nWhy: Self-identifying gaps before a notified body does so protects our market reputation and avoids the compounding cost of a formal non-conformity proceeding. (25 words)\n\nTotal: 5 + 39 + 35 + 25 = 104. Good.\n\nNow let me write my reasoning in 3-5 sentences as Compliance Officer:\n\nAs Compliance Officer, I read Article 12